 Text Pooling and AdaLN Conditioning \n87\t A.2 Backbone and CPE-MoE Configuration \n88\t\n89\t B Structured Prompt Interface \n90\t\n91\t B.1 Schema and Field Semantics \n92\t B.2 Natural-to-Structured Conversion \n93\t B.3 Public-Benchmark Prompt Rendering \n94\t B.4 Prompt Templates for Unified Audio Scenes \n95\t B.5 Representative Structured Prompts \n96\t\n97\t C Training and Inference Details \n98\t\n99\t C.1 Training Setup \n100\t C.2 Conditioning Dropout and Reference Masking \n101\t C.3 Adaptive Projected Guidance \n102\t C.4 Sampling Protocol \n103\t\n104\t D Evaluation Protocols \n105\t\n106\t D.1 Public-Benchmark Protocols \n107\t D.2 Complex-Scene Suite \n108\t D.3 Reference-Free Judge Rubric \n109\t D.4 Human Listening Study \n110\t\n111\t E Targeted Routing Ablations \n112\t\n113\t E.1 Scope and Evaluation Protocol \n114\t E.2 Fixed Prior–Evidence Fusion \n115\t E.3 Chunk-Size Sensitivity \n116\t\n117\t F Additional Routing Analyses \n118\t\n119\t F.1 Routing-Analysis Protocol \n120\t F.2 Phase- and Content-Conditioned Expert Usage \n121\t F.3 Does the Gate Control the Effective Router? \n122\t F.4 Representative Mixed-Scene Routing Case \n123\t\n124\t License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license\n125\t\n126\tarXiv:2608.09571v1 [cs.SD] 10 Aug 2026 \n127\t\n128\t SonicWeave: Chunk-Routed Mixture-of-Experts for Unified \n129\t Audio Scene Generation \n130\t\n131\tYunrui Cai 1,2,* , Xu Li 1, † \\dagger , Yucheng Zhou 1 , Jinchao Li 1 , Dingdong Wang 2 , Dongchao Yang 2 , \n132\t Xixin Wu 2 , Chen Zhang 1 , Zhiyong Wu 3 , Pengfei Wan 1 , Helen Meng 2, † \\dagger \n133\t\n134\t 1 Kling Team, Kuaishou Technology \n135\t 2 The Chinese University of Hong Kong\n136\t 3 Shenzhen International Graduate School, Tsinghua University\n137\t {yrcai, hmmeng}@se.cuhk.edu.hk, lixu15@kuaishou.com \n138\t\n139\t Abstract \n140\t Text-conditioned general audio generation is moving beyond isolated speech, music, and sound-effect synthesis toward a single model that can compose them into controllable, coherent audio scenes. This unified setting is particularly challenging: heterogeneous components impose conflicting structural requirements on a shared backbone, while a complex mixed scene may contain locally distinct or overlapping content that demands fine-grained adaptation within the same clip. Existing audio mixture-of-experts (MoEs) mainly route at the domain level, while token-wise routing overlooks the local continuity inherent to acoustic signals. We propose SonicWeave, a flow-matching model for unified audio scene generation. At its core is a chunk-routed MoE with a conflict-gated prior–evidence routing mechanism (CPE-MoE). CPE-MoE routes contiguous acoustic chunks by combining a global prior that encodes the structured text condition and diffusion phase with local evidence from the evolving acoustic state. A learned conflict gate favors the prior when local states are unreliable, while allowing local evidence to influence routing when a region departs from the global scene context. SonicWeave supports speech, music, sound effects, singing, and their fine-grained mixtures with a single set of weights.\n141\tAcross TTS, TTA, and TTM benchmarks, SonicWeave consistently improves over controlled Dense and Base-MoE baselines. Complex-scene evaluation further demonstrates improved compositional quality, while routing analyses reveal content-dependent expert specialization across diffusion phases. These results suggest that temporally coherent, prior–evidence routing is an effective conditional-computation strategy for unified audio generation. Project page: https://caiyunrui.github.io/SonicWeave . \n142\t\n143\t 1 1 footnotetext: Work done during an internship at Kling Team, Kuaishou Technology. 2 2 footnotetext: Corresponding authors. \n144\t\n145\t 1 Introduction \n146\t\n147\t Figure 1: Overview of SonicWeave for unified audio scene generation. Natural-language descriptions are converted into structured captions that separate speech content, vocal attributes, music, sound effects, and ambience. A single model then synthesizes these heterogeneous components jointly, while its CPE-MoE module provides conflict-gated, content-aware routing across acoustic chunks. \n148\t\n149\t Recent diffusion and flow-matching models  [ 21 ] have broadened text-conditioned synthesis across speech, music, and environmental sounds [ 20 , 5 , 17 ] . The next step is general audio generation : one model should not only cover these domains, but also compose them into coherent scenes and respond to structured specifications of heterogeneous acoustic content—for example, narration combined with music, ambience, and transient sound events [ 23 , 41 , 36 , 26 ] . This capability is important for moving from isolated audio generation to controllable modeling of real acoustic environments. \n150\t\n151\t This goal exposes a fundamental conditional-computation problem at two granularities: across domains (speech vs. music) and within a scene (locally overlapping components). Across domains, speech requires linguistic precision, music requires harmonic and rhythmic structure, and environmental sound includes both stationary textures and transient events. Within a single complex scene, these components may overlap or alternate, so different local regions require different computation and fine-grained control of heterogeneous content. A dense diffusion transformer (DiT) applies the same feed-forward transformation to every acoustic token, while existing audio MoEs primarily target domain-level specialization [ 24 ] . Token-wise routing may also vary unnecessarily across correlated neighboring frames, and local acoustic states are unreliable at early noisy diffusion phases. A useful unified generator therefore needs to coordinate global scene intent, local acoustic evidence, and temporal continuity rather than treating routing as an independent decision for every token. \n152\t\n153\t Acoustic latents exhibit strong short-range dependence: neighboring frames typically belong to the same phonetic unit, musical texture, or environmental event, even when the broader scene contains heterogeneous components. Chunk-level routing therefore occupies a useful middle ground between clip-level routing, which cannot adapt within a scene, and frame-level routing, which may fragment locally coherent structure. \n154\t\n155\t We propose SonicWeave, a unified flow-matching model designed for complex-scene generation with fine-grained local adaptation. Its core CPE-MoE module—Conflict-gated Prior–Evidence Mixture-of-Experts—routes contiguous acoustic chunks rather than independent frames. For each chunk, CPE-MoE combines a global prior from structured scene text and diffusion phase with local evidence from the evolving acoustic representation.\n156\tThe learned conflict gate favors the prior when local states are unreliable and shifts toward local evidence when a region contains content not explained by the global scene context.\n157\tText and time tokens remain on a shared expert, preserving a stable conditioning pathway. SonicWeave is trained over speech, music, sound effects, singing, and mixed scenes under one structured textual interface. \n158\t\n159\t Experiments show that SonicWeave performs strongly across TTS, TTA, and TTM while consistently improving over controlled Dense and Base-MoE baselines. On controlled complex scenes, it produces more coherent mixtures and better follows structured specifications of foreground, background, and speaker or vocal attributes. Routing analyses further show content-dependent expert specialization and diffusion-phase-adaptive use of the global prior and local evidence. \n160\t\n161\t Our contributions are threefold: \n162\t\n163\t • \n164\t\n165\t We identify conditional computation at two coupled granularities—across audio domains and within heterogeneous scenes—and formulate routing requirements that jointly account for global scene intent, local acoustic state, and short-range temporal continuity. \n166\t\n167\t • \n168\t\n169\t We introduce CPE-MoE, which routes contiguous acoustic chunks using a diffusion-phase-aware text prior, local acoustic evidence, and a learned conflict gate, while keeping text and time tokens on a stable shared path. \n170\t\n171\t • \n172\t\n173\t Through matched Dense and token-routed MoE controls, public-task benchmarks, a 100-prompt complex-scene suite, human listening tests, and routing analyses, we show that the proposed routing improves compositional fidelity without sacrificing perceptual quality. \n174\t\n175\t 2 Related Work \n176\t\n177\t Figure 2: SonicWeave framework. Left : a stereo VAE encodes audio into continuous latents, which are jointly modeled with a structured-caption stream in a flow-matching DiT. Right : the CPE-MoE module groups audio tokens into chunks and fuses a global text-and-phase prior with local acoustic evidence for sparse expert routing. \n178\t\n179\t 2.1 General and Unified Audio Generation \n180\t\n181\t Text-conditioned audio generation has progressed from autoregressive audio language models to diffusion and flow-matching systems for speech, music, and environmental sounds [ 22 , 20 , 5 , 17 ] . Unified models extend this scope through shared representations or architectures [ 23 , 41 , 36 , 30 ] . Dasheng AudioGen further targets coherent mixed scenes with structured multi-view captions [ 26 ] .\n182\tIn contrast, we focus on conditional computation within a unified continuous generator, where routing must resolve both heterogeneous audio domains and local compositional conflicts among coexisting scene components. \n183\t\n184\t 2.2 Structured Text Conditioning \n185\t\n186\t Structured conditions improve controllability by exposing event, temporal, or semantic factors beyond a single free-form caption [ 16 , 13 , 18 ] . Our structured caption normalizes heterogeneous annotations into one interface for speech content and attributes, music, sound events, and ambience. Unlike explicit timeline planning, it supplies a common scene description; CPE-MoE, the core module of SonicWeave, uses this global information as a routing prior and combines it with evolving local acoustic evidence. \n187\t\n188\t 2.3 Mixture-of-Experts for Diffusion and Audio Generation \n189\t\n190\t MoE enables conditional capacity through sparse expert activation and has been adapted to diffusion transformers [ 10 , 11 , 28 , 34 ] . In unified audio generation, UniMoE-Audio uses specialist-initialized experts and dynamic allocation to mitigate speech–music task conflict [ 24 ] . These approaches establish the value of specialization, but operate at task/modality granularity or route individual tokens. SonicWeave instead uses CPE-MoE to route contiguous acoustic chunks with a conflict gate between a global text-and-phase prior and local evidence, targeting stable, fine-grained specialization in heterogeneous mixed scenes. \n191\t\n192\t 3 Method \n193\t\n194\t 3.1 Overview \n195\t\n196\t Our dual-stream conditional flow-matching generator, SonicWeave, jointly models audio and text. In the audio stream, a stereo waveform is encoded by a pretrained stereo VAE into continuous audio latents; the model denoises a noised latent together with an optional masked reference for continuation and inpainting. In the text stream, a frozen language encoder embeds a structured audio caption. A joint audio–text diffusion transformer (DiT) then predicts the flow velocity, with the CPE-MoE module replacing the FFN in its final layers. Figure  2 summarizes the SonicWeave architecture and its CPE-MoE block. \n197\t\n198\t 3.2 Structured Text Conditioning \n199\t\n200\t We convert each natural-language annotation into a compact, audio-only structured caption. An automatic annotation pipeline first removes non-acoustic details and extracts a fixed set of fields: type, language, speech text or lyrics, speaker counts, speaker and vocal attributes, music, sound effect, ambience, and recording texture. The fields are serialized with explicit tags, e.g., Summary sentence. <type>speech</type> <lang>en</lang> <speech>...</speech> <music>...</music> . \n201\t\n202\t This factorized format separates otherwise entangled scene attributes, preserves linguistic content verbatim, and supplies a consistent interface across speech, music, sound effects, and mixed audio. The current schema explicitly supports up to two speakers for dialogue-oriented scenes. A frozen language encoder maps the resulting caption into text tokens for the DiT; we use one shared encoder rather than domain-specific conditioning modules. \n203\t\n204\t 3.3 Joint Audio–Text DiT Backbone \n205\t\n206\t Sequence Construction. \n207\t\n208\t Each transformer layer operates on the concatenation of three modality groups: \n209\t\n210\t 𝐡 = [ 𝐭 time ⏟ 1 ​ ‖ 𝐓 1 : L ⏟ text ‖ ​ 𝐀 1 : T ⏟ audio ] ∈ ℝ ( 1 + L + T ) × d . \\mathbf{h}=\\big[\\underbrace{\\mathbf{t}_{\\text{time}}}_{1}\\;\\|\\;\\underbrace{\\mathbf{T}_{1:L}}_{\\text{text}}\\;\\|\\;\\underbrace{\\mathbf{A}_{1:T}}_{\\text{audio}}\\big]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{(1+L+T)\\times d}. \n211\t\n212\t (1) \n213\t\n214\t The time token 𝐭 time \\mathbf{t}_{\\text{time}} is obtained by summing sinusoidal embeddings for diffusion time t t and segment start s s , then projecting the result to d d . Text tokens are 𝐓 = W cap ​ 𝐄 \\mathbf{T}=W_{\\text{cap}}\\mathbf{E} , where 𝐄 \\mathbf{E} denotes the frozen language-encoder outputs. Audio tokens concatenate the noised stereo-VAE latent 𝐱 t \\mathbf{x}_{t} with the masked reference 𝐜 \\mathbf{c} , followed by linear projection and convolutional positional embedding. \n215\t\n216\t Modality-Decoupled RoPE. \n217\t\n218\t Inspired by the modality-specific coordinate design of M-RoPE [ 37 ] , we assign text and audio independent one-dimensional position axes. The time token uses position 0 , while text and audio positions are reset separately: \n219\t\n220\t pos ⁡ ( 𝐭 time ) = 0 , pos ⁡ ( 𝐓 i ) = i , pos ⁡ ( 𝐀 j ) = j . \\operatorname{pos}(\\mathbf{t}_{\\mathrm{time}})=0,\\ \\operatorname{pos}(\\mathbf{T}_{i})=i,\\ \\operatorname{pos}(\\mathbf{A}_{j})=j. \n221\t\n222\t (2) \n223\t\n224\t With this modality-specific coordinate system, positional encoding preserves the intrinsic order of both text and audio while keeping their coordinates independent of sequence length in the other modality. It also provides a consistent relative-position structure for joint attention to model cross-modal correspondences. \n225\t\n226\t Text-Conditioned AdaLN. \n227\t\n228\t Joint self-attention provides token-level text–audio interaction, while adaptive normalization offers a complementary path for injecting global conditions into diffusion transformers [ 29 ] .\n229\tWe additionally use phase-aware attention pooling over the structured\n230\tcaption to provide global AdaLN conditioning; details are given in the\n231\tappendix. \n232\t\n233\t Cross-Modal Alignment Loss. \n234\t\n235\t The shared DiT processes audio and text jointly, but flow matching alone does not explicitly require their final hidden states to be aligned. We therefore add a symmetric in-batch contrastive objective, following the paired-representation alignment principle used in CLIP [ 31 ] . Let 𝐚 b \\mathbf{a}_{b} and 𝐫 b \\mathbf{r}_{b} be the ℓ 2 \\ell_{2} -normalized, mask-pooled audio and text states of sample b b , respectively, and define S b ​ b ′ = exp ⁡ ( λ ) ​ 𝐚 b ⊤ ​ 𝐫 b ′ S_{bb^{\\prime}}=\\exp(\\lambda)\\,\\mathbf{a}_{b}^{\\top}\\mathbf{r}_{b^{\\prime}} , where λ \\lambda is learned. We minimize \n236\t\n237\t ℒ CL = 1 2 ​ [ CE ⁡ ( 𝐒 , 𝐲 ) + CE ⁡ ( 𝐒 ⊤ , 𝐲 ) ] , y b = b . \\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{CL}}=\\tfrac{1}{2}\\left[\\operatorname{CE}(\\mathbf{S},\\mathbf{y})+\\operatorname{CE}(\\mathbf{S}^{\\top},\\mathbf{y})\\right],\\ y_{b}=b. \n238\t\n239\t (3) \n240\t\n241\t This aligns paired audio–caption representations and supports the text-derived prior and audio-derived evidence used by CPE-MoE. \n242\t\n243\t 3.4 CPE-MoE: Conflict-Gated Prior–Evidence Chunk Routing \n244\t\n245\t The CPE-MoE module replaces the dense FFN in the final transformer layers with shared and sparsely routed experts, providing conditional capacity for heterogeneous audio. As shown in Figure  2 , it learns routing from two complementary sources: structured text and diffusion phase specify global generation intent, while evolving audio states reveal local acoustic content. Routing contiguous chunks preserves short-range audio continuity, allowing expert specialization across broad tasks and locally mixed content under one objective. \n246\t\n247\t Modality-Decoupled Dispatch. \n248\t\n249\t Given the layer input 𝐡 ∈ ℝ B × ( 1 + L + T ) × d \\mathbf{h}\\!\\in\\!\\mathbb{R}^{B\\times(1+L+T)\\times d} , we split it into a context group (time and text tokens) and an audio group: \n250\t\n251\t 𝐡 ctx = 𝐡 [ : , : 1 + L ] , 𝐡 aud = 𝐡 [ : , − T : ] . \\mathbf{h}_{\\text{ctx}}=\\mathbf{h}_{[:,\\,:1{+}L]},\\ \\mathbf{h}_{\\text{aud}}=\\mathbf{h}_{[:,\\,-T:]}. \n252\t\n253\t (4) \n254\t\n255\t The context group is processed only by the shared expert 𝐨 ctx = MLP shared ​ ( 𝐡 ctx ) \\mathbf{o}_{\\text{ctx}}=\\mathrm{MLP}_{\\text{shared}}(\\mathbf{h}_{\\text{ctx}}) . This isolates the text and time representations from the routing gate. \n256\t\n257\t Chunk-Level Routing. \n258\t\n259\t We partition the T T audio states into N c = ⌈ T / C ⌉ N_{c}=\\lceil T/C\\rceil non-overlapping chunks { 𝒞 j } j = 1 N c \\{\\mathcal{C}_{j}\\}_{j=1}^{N_{c}} of at most C C consecutive frames. Let m i ∈ { 0 , 1 } m_{i}\\in\\{0,1\\} indicate whether frame i i is valid. The pooled state of chunk j j is \n260\t\n261\t 𝐞 j = ∑ i ∈ 𝒞 j m i ​ 𝐡 aud , i max ⁡ ( 1 , ∑ i ∈ 𝒞 j m i ) . \\mathbf{e}_{j}=\\frac{\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{C}_{j}}m_{i}\\mathbf{h}_{\\mathrm{aud},i}}{\\max\\!\\left(1,\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{C}_{j}}m_{i}\\right)}. \n262\t\n263\t (5) \n264\t\n265\t Padding-only chunks are excluded from routing and auxiliary statistics. The prior–evidence router makes one top- K K assignment per valid chunk and broadcasts it to all frames in that chunk. The selected experts nevertheless transform the original frame states, not the pooled state 𝐞 j \\mathbf{e}_{j} . Thus, pooling controls only expert selection: frame-level modeling is retained while neighboring frames share a locally consistent computation path.\n266\tImportantly, the chunk is the routing unit rather than the representation unit: expert selection is shared within a chunk, but each expert still transforms the original frame-level states. \n267\t\n268\t Prior and Evidence Signals. \n269\t\n270\t The router combines two complementary signals with different information scopes for each chunk. \n271\t\n272\t Prior. A global, chunk-independent signal that summarizes what to generate and at which diffusion phase : \n273\t\n274\t 𝐩 = [ 𝐭 time ∥ 𝐓 ¯ ] ∈ ℝ 2 ​ d , ℓ prior = W prior ​ ( 𝐩 ) ∈ ℝ N e , \\mathbf{p}=[\\mathbf{t}_{\\text{time}}\\;\\|\\;\\bar{\\mathbf{T}}]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2d},\\ \\boldsymbol{\\ell}^{\\mathrm{prior}}=W_{\\mathrm{prior}}(\\mathbf{p})\\in\\mathbb{R}^{N_{e}}, \n275\t\n276\t (6) \n277\t\n278\t where 𝐓 ¯ \\bar{\\mathbf{T}} is the mask-pooled text hidden state at the current layer. Because 𝐩 \\mathbf{p} carries both the text summary and the diffusion-step embedding, ℓ prior \\boldsymbol{\\ell}^{\\mathrm{prior}} is a task- and step-aware expert preference that summarizes the global generation intent. \n279\t\n280\t Evidence. A local signal is computed from the current context-conditioned audio representation 𝐞 j \\mathbf{e}_{j} : \n281\t\n282\t ℓ j evid = W evid ​ ( 𝐞 j ) ∈ ℝ N e . \\boldsymbol{\\ell}^{\\mathrm{evid}}_{j}=W_{\\mathrm{evid}}(\\mathbf{e}_{j})\\in\\mathbb{R}^{N_{e}}. \n283\t\n284\t (7) \n285\t\n286\t Because 𝐞 j \\mathbf{e}_{j} is pooled after joint self-attention, it reflects the acoustic state currently emerging in chunk j j . The evidence logits can thus capture local departures from the scene-level description, such as a transient event within speech or music. \n287\t\n288\t Both projections are 2-layer MLPs with SiLU activation and zero-initialized output biases, avoiding an explicit expert preference at initialization. \n289\t\n290\t Conflict Gate and Fusion. \n291\t\n292\t Table 1: Comparison with representative specialized and general-purpose audio generators. ✓ \\checkmark indicates explicitly reported support; △ \\triangle denotes partial support, limited public demonstrations or support under restricted settings; and – denotes no explicit evidence of support found in the public documentation we reviewed. \"Background\" includes music, sound effects, environmental ambience, or their combinations. \n293\t\n294\t Category \n295\t Model \n296\t General tasks \n297\t Complex-scene generation \n298\t\n299\t TTS \n300\t TTA \n301\t TTM \n302\t Speech+Bg. \n303\t Sing+Bg. \n304\t Dialog \n305\t Dialog+Bg. \n306\t\n307\t Specialized \n308\t CosyVoice 2  [ 8 ] \n309\t\n310\t ✓ \\checkmark \n311\t – \n312\t – \n313\t – \n314\t – \n315\t – \n316\t – \n317\t\n318\t F5-TTS  [ 5 ] \n319\t\n320\t ✓ \\checkmark \n321\t – \n322\t – \n323\t – \n324\t – \n325\t – \n326\t – \n327\t\n328\t Step-Audio  [ 15 ] \n329\t\n330\t ✓ \\checkmark \n331\t – \n332\t – \n333\t – \n334\t – \n335\t △ \\triangle \n336\t – \n337\t\n338\t TangoFlux  [ 17 ] \n339\t\n340\t – \n341\t ✓ \\checkmark \n342\t – \n343\t – \n344\t – \n345\t – \n346\t – \n347\t\n348\t MusicGen-Large  [ 7 ] \n349\t\n350\t – \n351\t – \n352\t ✓ \\checkmark \n353\t – \n354\t – \n355\t – \n356\t – \n357\t\n358\t SegTune  [ 38 ] \n359\t\n360\t – \n361\t – \n362\t ✓ \\checkmark \n363\t – \n364\t △ \\triangle \n365\t – \n366\t – \n367\t\n368\t General / multi-task \n369\t Qwen2.5-Omni  [ 39 ] \n370\t\n371\t ✓ \\checkmark \n372\t △ \\triangle \n373\t △ \\triangle \n374\t – \n375\t – \n376\t △ \\triangle \n377\t – \n378\t\n379\t AudioLDM 2  [ 23 ] \n380\t\n381\t △ \\triangle \n382\t ✓ \\checkmark \n383\t △ \\triangle \n384\t △ \\triangle \n385\t △ \\triangle \n386\t – \n387\t – \n388\t\n389\t Stable Audio Open  [ 9 ] \n390\t\n391\t – \n392\t ✓ \\checkmark \n393\t ✓ \\checkmark \n394\t – \n395\t △ \\triangle \n396\t – \n397\t – \n398\t\n399\t UniAudio  [ 41 ] \n400\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6b970116-bc68-429c-9d83-8e23452428b2","uuid":"31ee4e5b-8630-4f82-9628-7553ba122826","timestamp":"2026-08-11T06:47:11.104Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/sonicweave.txt","content":" \n\n SonicWeave: Chunk-Routed Mixture-of-Experts for Unified Audio Scene Generation \n\n Report GitHub Issue \n × \n\n Title: \n\n Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: \n\n Description: \n\n Submit without GitHub \n Submit in GitHub \n\n arXiv is now an independent nonprofit! \n Learn more \n × \n\n Back to arXiv \n\n Why HTML? \n\n Report Issue \n\n Back to Abstract \n\n Download PDF \n\n Abstract \n 1 Introduction \n\n 2 Related Work \n\n 2.1 General and Unified Audio Generation \n 2.2 Structured Text Conditioning \n 2.3 Mixture-of-Experts for Diffusion and Audio Generation \n\n 3 Method \n\n 3.1 Overview \n 3.2 Structured Text Conditioning \n\n 3.3 Joint Audio–Text DiT Backbone \n\n Sequence Construction. \n Modality-Decoupled RoPE. \n Text-Conditioned AdaLN. \n Cross-Modal Alignment Loss. \n\n 3.4 CPE-MoE: Conflict-Gated Prior–Evidence Chunk Routing \n\n Modality-Decoupled Dispatch. \n Chunk-Level Routing. \n Prior and Evidence Signals. \n Conflict Gate and Fusion. \n Sparse Dispatch and Composition. \n Load Balancing. \n\n 3.5 Conditional Flow-Matching Training \n 3.6 Inference \n\n 4 Experiments \n\n 4.1 Experimental Setup \n\n Tasks and Benchmarks. \n Baselines. \n Metrics. \n Implementation Details. \n\n 4.2 Results on Public Benchmarks \n 4.3 Complex-Scene Evaluation \n\n 4.4 Routing Analysis and Visualization \n\n Content-conditioned expert specialization. \n Phase-dependent conflict gating. \n\n 5 Conclusion \n References \n\n A Architecture and Objective Details \n\n A.1 Phase-Aware Text Pooling and AdaLN Conditioning \n A.2 Backbone and CPE-MoE Configuration \n\n B Structured Prompt Interface \n\n B.1 Schema and Field Semantics \n B.2 Natural-to-Structured Conversion \n B.3 Public-Benchmark Prompt Rendering \n B.4 Prompt Templates for Unified Audio Scenes \n B.5 Representative Structured Prompts \n\n C Training and Inference Details \n\n C.1 Training Setup \n C.2 Conditioning Dropout and Reference Masking \n C.3 Adaptive Projected Guidance \n C.4 Sampling Protocol \n\n D Evaluation Protocols \n\n D.1 Public-Benchmark Protocols \n D.2 Complex-Scene Suite \n D.3 Reference-Free Judge Rubric \n D.4 Human Listening Study \n\n E Targeted Routing Ablations \n\n E.1 Scope and Evaluation Protocol \n E.2 Fixed Prior–Evidence Fusion \n E.3 Chunk-Size Sensitivity \n\n F Additional Routing Analyses \n\n F.1 Routing-Analysis Protocol \n F.2 Phase- and Content-Conditioned Expert Usage \n F.3 Does the Gate Control the Effective Router? \n F.4 Representative Mixed-Scene Routing Case \n\n License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license\n\narXiv:2608.09571v1 [cs.SD] 10 Aug 2026 \n\n SonicWeave: Chunk-Routed Mixture-of-Experts for Unified \n Audio Scene Generation \n\nYunrui Cai 1,2,* , Xu Li 1, † \\dagger , Yucheng Zhou 1 , Jinchao Li 1 , Dingdong Wang 2 , Dongchao Yang 2 , \n Xixin Wu 2 , Chen Zhang 1 , Zhiyong Wu 3 , Pengfei Wan 1 , Helen Meng 2, † \\dagger \n\n 1 Kling Team, Kuaishou Technology \n 2 The Chinese University of Hong Kong\n 3 Shenzhen International Graduate School, Tsinghua University\n {yrcai, hmmeng}@se.cuhk.edu.hk, lixu15@kuaishou.com \n\n Abstract \n Text-conditioned general audio generation is moving beyond isolated speech, music, and sound-effect synthesis toward a single model that can compose them into controllable, coherent audio scenes. This unified setting is particularly challenging: heterogeneous components impose conflicting structural requirements on a shared backbone, while a complex mixed scene may contain locally distinct or overlapping content that demands fine-grained adaptation within the same clip. Existing audio mixture-of-experts (MoEs) mainly route at the domain level, while token-wise routing overlooks the local continuity inherent to acoustic signals. We propose SonicWeave, a flow-matching model for unified audio scene generation. At its core is a chunk-routed MoE with a conflict-gated prior–evidence routing mechanism (CPE-MoE). CPE-MoE routes contiguous acoustic chunks by combining a global prior that encodes the structured text condition and diffusion phase with local evidence from the evolving acoustic state. A learned conflict gate favors the prior when local states are unreliable, while allowing local evidence to influence routing when a region departs from the global scene context. SonicWeave supports speech, music, sound effects, singing, and their fine-grained mixtures with a single set of weights.\nAcross TTS, TTA, and TTM benchmarks, SonicWeave consistently improves over controlled Dense and Base-MoE baselines. Complex-scene evaluation further demonstrates improved compositional quality, while routing analyses reveal content-dependent expert specialization across diffusion phases. These results suggest that temporally coherent, prior–evidence routing is an effective conditional-computation strategy for unified audio generation. Project page: https://caiyunrui.github.io/SonicWeave . \n\n 1 1 footnotetext: Work done during an internship at Kling Team, Kuaishou Technology. 2 2 footnotetext: Corresponding authors. \n\n 1 Introduction \n\n Figure 1: Overview of SonicWeave for unified audio scene generation. Natural-language descriptions are converted into structured captions that separate speech content, vocal attributes, music, sound effects, and ambience. A single model then synthesizes these heterogeneous components jointly, while its CPE-MoE module provides conflict-gated, content-aware routing across acoustic chunks. \n\n Recent diffusion and flow-matching models  [ 21 ] have broadened text-conditioned synthesis across speech, music, and environmental sounds [ 20 , 5 , 17 ] . The next step is general audio generation : one model should not only cover these domains, but also compose them into coherent scenes and respond to structured specifications of heterogeneous acoustic content—for example, narration combined with music, ambience, and transient sound events [ 23 , 41 , 36 , 26 ] . This capability is important for moving from isolated audio generation to controllable modeling of real acoustic environments. \n\n This goal exposes a fundamental conditional-computation problem at two granularities: across domains (speech vs. music) and within a scene (locally overlapping components). Across domains, speech requires linguistic precision, music requires harmonic and rhythmic structure, and environmental sound includes both stationary textures and transient events. Within a single complex scene, these components may overlap or alternate, so different local regions require different computation and fine-grained control of heterogeneous content. A dense diffusion transformer (DiT) applies the same feed-forward transformation to every acoustic token, while existing audio MoEs primarily target domain-level specialization [ 24 ] . Token-wise routing may also vary unnecessarily across correlated neighboring frames, and local acoustic states are unreliable at early noisy diffusion phases. A useful unified generator therefore needs to coordinate global scene intent, local acoustic evidence, and temporal continuity rather than treating routing as an independent decision for every token. \n\n Acoustic latents exhibit strong short-range dependence: neighboring frames typically belong to the same phonetic unit, musical texture, or environmental event, even when the broader scene contains heterogeneous components. Chunk-level routing therefore occupies a useful middle ground between clip-level routing, which cannot adapt within a scene, and frame-level routing, which may fragment locally coherent structure. \n\n We propose SonicWeave, a unified flow-matching model designed for complex-scene generation with fine-grained local adaptation. Its core CPE-MoE module—Conflict-gated Prior–Evidence Mixture-of-Experts—routes contiguous acoustic chunks rather than independent frames. For each chunk, CPE-MoE combines a global prior from structured scene text and diffusion phase with local evidence from the evolving acoustic representation.\nThe learned conflict gate favors the prior when local states are unreliable and shifts toward local evidence when a region contains content not explained by the global scene context.\nText and time tokens remain on a shared expert, preserving a stable conditioning pathway. SonicWeave is trained over speech, music, sound effects, singing, and mixed scenes under one structured textual interface. \n\n Experiments show that SonicWeave performs strongly across TTS, TTA, and TTM while consistently improving over controlled Dense and Base-MoE baselines. On controlled complex scenes, it produces more coherent mixtures and better follows structured specifications of foreground, background, and speaker or vocal attributes. Routing analyses further show content-dependent expert specialization and diffusion-phase-adaptive use of the global prior and local evidence. \n\n Our contributions are threefold: \n\n • \n\n We identify conditional computation at two coupled granularities—across audio domains and within heterogeneous scenes—and formulate routing requirements that jointly account for global scene intent, local acoustic state, and short-range temporal continuity. \n\n • \n\n We introduce CPE-MoE, which routes contiguous acoustic chunks using a diffusion-phase-aware text prior, local acoustic evidence, and a learned conflict gate, while keeping text and time tokens on a stable shared path. \n\n • \n\n Through matched Dense and token-routed MoE controls, public-task benchmarks, a 100-prompt complex-scene suite, human listening tests, and routing analyses, we show that the proposed routing improves compositional fidelity without sacrificing perceptual quality. \n\n 2 Related Work \n\n Figure 2: SonicWeave framework. Left : a stereo VAE encodes audio into continuous latents, which are jointly modeled with a structured-caption stream in a flow-matching DiT. Right : the CPE-MoE module groups audio tokens into chunks and fuses a global text-and-phase prior with local acoustic evidence for sparse expert routing. \n\n 2.1 General and Unified Audio Generation \n\n Text-conditioned audio generation has progressed from autoregressive audio language models to diffusion and flow-matching systems for speech, music, and environmental sounds [ 22 , 20 , 5 , 17 ] . Unified models extend this scope through shared representations or architectures [ 23 , 41 , 36 , 30 ] . Dasheng AudioGen further targets coherent mixed scenes with structured multi-view captions [ 26 ] .\nIn contrast, we focus on conditional computation within a unified continuous generator, where routing must resolve both heterogeneous audio domains and local compositional conflicts among coexisting scene components. \n\n 2.2 Structured Text Conditioning \n\n Structured conditions improve controllability by exposing event, temporal, or semantic factors beyond a single free-form caption [ 16 , 13 , 18 ] . Our structured caption normalizes heterogeneous annotations into one interface for speech content and attributes, music, sound events, and ambience. Unlike explicit timeline planning, it supplies a common scene description; CPE-MoE, the core module of SonicWeave, uses this global information as a routing prior and combines it with evolving local acoustic evidence. \n\n 2.3 Mixture-of-Experts for Diffusion and Audio Generation \n\n MoE enables conditional capacity through sparse expert activation and has been adapted to diffusion transformers [ 10 , 11 , 28 , 34 ] . In unified audio generation, UniMoE-Audio uses specialist-initialized experts and dynamic allocation to mitigate speech–music task conflict [ 24 ] . These approaches establish the value of specialization, but operate at task/modality granularity or route individual tokens. SonicWeave instead uses CPE-MoE to route contiguous acoustic chunks with a conflict gate between a global text-and-phase prior and local evidence, targeting stable, fine-grained specialization in heterogeneous mixed scenes. \n\n 3 Method \n\n 3.1 Overview \n\n Our dual-stream conditional flow-matching generator, SonicWeave, jointly models audio and text. In the audio stream, a stereo waveform is encoded by a pretrained stereo VAE into continuous audio latents; the model denoises a noised latent together with an optional masked reference for continuation and inpainting. In the text stream, a frozen language encoder embeds a structured audio caption. A joint audio–text diffusion transformer (DiT) then predicts the flow velocity, with the CPE-MoE module replacing the FFN in its final layers. Figure  2 summarizes the SonicWeave architecture and its CPE-MoE block. \n\n 3.2 Structured Text Conditioning \n\n We convert each natural-language annotation into a compact, audio-only structured caption. An automatic annotation pipeline first removes non-acoustic details and extracts a fixed set of fields: type, language, speech text or lyrics, speaker counts, speaker and vocal attributes, music, sound effect, ambience, and recording texture. The fields are serialized with explicit tags, e.g., Summary sentence. <type>speech</type> <lang>en</lang> <speech>...</speech> <music>...</music> . \n\n This factorized format separates otherwise entangled scene attributes, preserves linguistic content verbatim, and supplies a consistent interface across speech, music, sound effects, and mixed audio. The current schema explicitly supports up to two speakers for dialogue-oriented scenes. A frozen language encoder maps the resulting caption into text tokens for the DiT; we use one shared encoder rather than domain-specific conditioning modules. \n\n 3.3 Joint Audio–Text DiT Backbone \n\n Sequence Construction. \n\n Each transformer layer operates on the concatenation of three modality groups: \n\n 𝐡 = [ 𝐭 time ⏟ 1 ​ ‖ 𝐓 1 : L ⏟ text ‖ ​ 𝐀 1 : T ⏟ audio ] ∈ ℝ ( 1 + L + T ) × d . \\mathbf{h}=\\big[\\underbrace{\\mathbf{t}_{\\text{time}}}_{1}\\;\\|\\;\\underbrace{\\mathbf{T}_{1:L}}_{\\text{text}}\\;\\|\\;\\underbrace{\\mathbf{A}_{1:T}}_{\\text{audio}}\\big]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{(1+L+T)\\times d}. \n\n (1) \n\n The time token 𝐭 time \\mathbf{t}_{\\text{time}} is obtained by summing sinusoidal embeddings for diffusion time t t and segment start s s , then projecting the result to d d . Text tokens are 𝐓 = W cap ​ 𝐄 \\mathbf{T}=W_{\\text{cap}}\\mathbf{E} , where 𝐄 \\mathbf{E} denotes the frozen language-encoder outputs. Audio tokens concatenate the noised stereo-VAE latent 𝐱 t \\mathbf{x}_{t} with the masked reference 𝐜 \\mathbf{c} , followed by linear projection and convolutional positional embedding. \n\n Modality-Decoupled RoPE. \n\n Inspired by the modality-specific coordinate design of M-RoPE [ 37 ] , we assign text and audio independent one-dimensional position axes. The time token uses position 0 , while text and audio positions are reset separately: \n\n pos ⁡ ( 𝐭 time ) = 0 , pos ⁡ ( 𝐓 i ) = i , pos ⁡ ( 𝐀 j ) = j . \\operatorname{pos}(\\mathbf{t}_{\\mathrm{time}})=0,\\ \\operatorname{pos}(\\mathbf{T}_{i})=i,\\ \\operatorname{pos}(\\mathbf{A}_{j})=j. \n\n (2) \n\n With this modality-specific coordinate system, positional encoding preserves the intrinsic order of both text and audio while keeping their coordinates independent of sequence length in the other modality. It also provides a consistent relative-position structure for joint attention to model cross-modal correspondences. \n\n Text-Conditioned AdaLN. \n\n Joint self-attention provides token-level text–audio interaction, while adaptive normalization offers a complementary path for injecting global conditions into diffusion transformers [ 29 ] .\nWe additionally use phase-aware attention pooling over the structured\ncaption to provide global AdaLN conditioning; details are given in the\nappendix. \n\n Cross-Modal Alignment Loss. \n\n The shared DiT processes audio and text jointly, but flow matching alone does not explicitly require their final hidden states to be aligned. We therefore add a symmetric in-batch contrastive objective, following the paired-representation alignment principle used in CLIP [ 31 ] . Let 𝐚 b \\mathbf{a}_{b} and 𝐫 b \\mathbf{r}_{b} be the ℓ 2 \\ell_{2} -normalized, mask-pooled audio and text states of sample b b , respectively, and define S b ​ b ′ = exp ⁡ ( λ ) ​ 𝐚 b ⊤ ​ 𝐫 b ′ S_{bb^{\\prime}}=\\exp(\\lambda)\\,\\mathbf{a}_{b}^{\\top}\\mathbf{r}_{b^{\\prime}} , where λ \\lambda is learned. We minimize \n\n ℒ CL = 1 2 ​ [ CE ⁡ ( 𝐒 , 𝐲 ) + CE ⁡ ( 𝐒 ⊤ , 𝐲 ) ] , y b = b . \\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{CL}}=\\tfrac{1}{2}\\left[\\operatorname{CE}(\\mathbf{S},\\mathbf{y})+\\operatorname{CE}(\\mathbf{S}^{\\top},\\mathbf{y})\\right],\\ y_{b}=b. \n\n (3) \n\n This aligns paired audio–caption representations and supports the text-derived prior and audio-derived evidence used by CPE-MoE. \n\n 3.4 CPE-MoE: Conflict-Gated Prior–Evidence Chunk Routing \n\n The CPE-MoE module replaces the dense FFN in the final transformer layers with shared and sparsely routed experts, providing conditional capacity for heterogeneous audio. As shown in Figure  2 , it learns routing from two complementary sources: structured text and diffusion phase specify global generation intent, while evolving audio states reveal local acoustic content. Routing contiguous chunks preserves short-range audio continuity, allowing expert specialization across broad tasks and locally mixed content under one objective. \n\n Modality-Decoupled Dispatch. \n\n Given the layer input 𝐡 ∈ ℝ B × ( 1 + L + T ) × d \\mathbf{h}\\!\\in\\!\\mathbb{R}^{B\\times(1+L+T)\\times d} , we split it into a context group (time and text tokens) and an audio group: \n\n 𝐡 ctx = 𝐡 [ : , : 1 + L ] , 𝐡 aud = 𝐡 [ : , − T : ] . \\mathbf{h}_{\\text{ctx}}=\\mathbf{h}_{[:,\\,:1{+}L]},\\ \\mathbf{h}_{\\text{aud}}=\\mathbf{h}_{[:,\\,-T:]}. \n\n (4) \n\n The context group is processed only by the shared expert 𝐨 ctx = MLP shared ​ ( 𝐡 ctx ) \\mathbf{o}_{\\text{ctx}}=\\mathrm{MLP}_{\\text{shared}}(\\mathbf{h}_{\\text{ctx}}) . This isolates the text and time representations from the routing gate. \n\n Chunk-Level Routing. \n\n We partition the T T audio states into N c = ⌈ T / C ⌉ N_{c}=\\lceil T/C\\rceil non-overlapping chunks { 𝒞 j } j = 1 N c \\{\\mathcal{C}_{j}\\}_{j=1}^{N_{c}} of at most C C consecutive frames. Let m i ∈ { 0 , 1 } m_{i}\\in\\{0,1\\} indicate whether frame i i is valid. The pooled state of chunk j j is \n\n 𝐞 j = ∑ i ∈ 𝒞 j m i ​ 𝐡 aud , i max ⁡ ( 1 , ∑ i ∈ 𝒞 j m i ) . \\mathbf{e}_{j}=\\frac{\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{C}_{j}}m_{i}\\mathbf{h}_{\\mathrm{aud},i}}{\\max\\!\\left(1,\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal{C}_{j}}m_{i}\\right)}. \n\n (5) \n\n Padding-only chunks are excluded from routing and auxiliary statistics. The prior–evidence router makes one top- K K assignment per valid chunk and broadcasts it to all frames in that chunk. The selected experts nevertheless transform the original frame states, not the pooled state 𝐞 j \\mathbf{e}_{j} . Thus, pooling controls only expert selection: frame-level modeling is retained while neighboring frames share a locally consistent computation path.\nImportantly, the chunk is the routing unit rather than the representation unit: expert selection is shared within a chunk, but each expert still transforms the original frame-level states. \n\n Prior and Evidence Signals. \n\n The router combines two complementary signals with different information scopes for each chunk. \n\n Prior. A global, chunk-independent signal that summarizes what to generate and at which diffusion phase : \n\n 𝐩 = [ 𝐭 time ∥ 𝐓 ¯ ] ∈ ℝ 2 ​ d , ℓ prior = W prior ​ ( 𝐩 ) ∈ ℝ N e , \\mathbf{p}=[\\mathbf{t}_{\\text{time}}\\;\\|\\;\\bar{\\mathbf{T}}]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2d},\\ \\boldsymbol{\\ell}^{\\mathrm{prior}}=W_{\\mathrm{prior}}(\\mathbf{p})\\in\\mathbb{R}^{N_{e}}, \n\n (6) \n\n where 𝐓 ¯ \\bar{\\mathbf{T}} is the mask-pooled text hidden state at the current layer. Because 𝐩 \\mathbf{p} carries both the text summary and the diffusion-step embedding, ℓ prior \\boldsymbol{\\ell}^{\\mathrm{prior}} is a task- and step-aware expert preference that summarizes the global generation intent. \n\n Evidence. A local signal is computed from the current context-conditioned audio representation 𝐞 j \\mathbf{e}_{j} : \n\n ℓ j evid = W evid ​ ( 𝐞 j ) ∈ ℝ N e . \\boldsymbol{\\ell}^{\\mathrm{evid}}_{j}=W_{\\mathrm{evid}}(\\mathbf{e}_{j})\\in\\mathbb{R}^{N_{e}}. \n\n (7) \n\n Because 𝐞 j \\mathbf{e}_{j} is pooled after joint self-attention, it reflects the acoustic state currently emerging in chunk j j . The evidence logits can thus capture local departures from the scene-level description, such as a transient event within speech or music. \n\n Both projections are 2-layer MLPs with SiLU activation and zero-initialized output biases, avoiding an explicit expert preference at initialization. \n\n Conflict Gate and Fusion. \n\n Table 1: Comparison with representative specialized and general-purpose audio generators. ✓ \\checkmark indicates explicitly reported support; △ \\triangle denotes partial support, limited public demonstrations or support under restricted settings; and – denotes no explicit evidence of support found in the public documentation we reviewed. \"Background\" includes music, sound effects, environmental ambience, or their combinations. \n\n Category \n Model \n General tasks \n Complex-scene generation \n\n TTS \n TTA \n TTM \n Speech+Bg. \n Sing+Bg. \n Dialog \n Dialog+Bg. \n\n Specialized \n CosyVoice 2  [ 8 ] \n\n ✓ \\checkmark \n – \n – \n – \n – \n – \n – \n\n F5-TTS  [ 5 ] \n\n ✓ \\checkmark \n – \n – \n – \n – \n – \n – \n\n Step-Audio  [ 15 ] \n\n ✓ \\checkmark \n – \n – \n – \n – \n △ \\triangle \n – \n\n TangoFlux  [ 17 ] \n\n – \n ✓ \\checkmark \n – \n – \n – \n – \n – \n\n MusicGen-Large  [ 7 ] \n\n – \n – \n ✓ \\checkmark \n – \n – \n – \n – \n\n SegTune  [ 38 ] \n\n – \n – \n ✓ \\checkmark \n – \n △ \\triangle \n – \n – \n\n General / multi-task \n Qwen2.5-Omni  [ 39 ] \n\n ✓ \\checkmark \n △ \\triangle \n △ \\triangle \n – \n – \n △ \\triangle \n – \n\n AudioLDM 2  [ 23 ] \n\n △ \\triangle \n ✓ \\checkmark \n △ \\triangle \n △ \\triangle \n △ \\triangle \n – \n – \n\n Stable Audio Open  [ 9 ] \n\n – \n ✓ \\checkmark \n ✓ \\checkmark \n – \n △ \\triangle \n – \n – \n\n UniAudio  [ 41 ] \n","numLines":400,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1960}}}
