{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a92482479a043b0d","type":"tool_result","content":"400\t\n401\t ✓ \\checkmark \n402\t ✓ \\checkmark \n403\t ✓ \\checkmark \n404\t △ \\triangle \n405\t – \n406\t – \n407\t – \n408\t\n409\t AudioX  [ 35 ] \n410\t\n411\t – \n412\t ✓ \\checkmark \n413\t ✓ \\checkmark \n414\t – \n415\t – \n416\t – \n417\t – \n418\t\n419\t Higgs Audio V2  [ 4 ] \n420\t\n421\t ✓ \\checkmark \n422\t ✓ \\checkmark \n423\t △ \\triangle \n424\t △ \\triangle \n425\t – \n426\t ✓ \\checkmark \n427\t △ \\triangle \n428\t\n429\t UniMoE-Audio  [ 24 ] \n430\t\n431\t ✓ \\checkmark \n432\t – \n433\t ✓ \\checkmark \n434\t – \n435\t – \n436\t – \n437\t – \n438\t\n439\t UniFlow-Audio  [ 40 ] \n440\t\n441\t ✓ \\checkmark \n442\t ✓ \\checkmark \n443\t ✓ \\checkmark \n444\t △ \\triangle \n445\t – \n446\t △ \\triangle \n447\t △ \\triangle \n448\t\n449\t Dasheng AudioGen  [ 26 ] \n450\t\n451\t ✓ \\checkmark \n452\t ✓ \\checkmark \n453\t ✓ \\checkmark \n454\t ✓ \\checkmark \n455\t – \n456\t △ \\triangle \n457\t △ \\triangle \n458\t\n459\t General / compositional \n460\t SonicWeave (ours) \n461\t ✓ \\checkmark \n462\t ✓ \\checkmark \n463\t ✓ \\checkmark \n464\t ✓ \\checkmark \n465\t ✓ \\checkmark \n466\t ✓ \\checkmark \n467\t ✓ \\checkmark \n468\t\n469\t The prior gives a global expert preference, but local audio becomes informative at different rates across diffusion phases and acoustic regions. We therefore let each chunk decide how far its routing should move from the prior toward the local evidence. We first project the semantic–phase summary into the d d -dimensional hidden space of the joint DiT: \n470\t\n471\t 𝐩 ~ = W align ​ 𝐩 ∈ ℝ d . \\tilde{\\mathbf{p}}=W_{\\mathrm{align}}\\mathbf{p}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}. \n472\t\n473\t (8) \n474\t\n475\t The gate observes both signals and their explicit pairwise interactions: \n476\t\n477\t ϕ j = LN ( [ 𝐩 ~ ∥ 𝐞 j ∥ 𝐩 ~ ⊙ 𝐞 j ∥ | 𝐩 ~ − 𝐞 j | ] ) , \\boldsymbol{\\phi}_{j}=\\mathrm{LN}\\!\\left([\\,\\tilde{\\mathbf{p}}\\;\\|\\;\\mathbf{e}_{j}\\;\\|\\;\\tilde{\\mathbf{p}}\\!\\odot\\!\\mathbf{e}_{j}\\;\\|\\;|\\tilde{\\mathbf{p}}-\\mathbf{e}_{j}|\\,]\\right), \n478\t\n479\t (9) \n480\t\n481\t g j = σ ​ ( MLP g ⁡ ( ϕ j ) ) . g_{j}=\\sigma\\!\\left(\\operatorname{MLP}_{g}(\\boldsymbol{\\phi}_{j})\\right). \n482\t\n483\t (10) \n484\t\n485\t Following pairwise matching features [ 6 ] , the product exposes coordinate-wise agreement and the absolute difference exposes disagreement, while the original vectors retain source-specific information. The MLP therefore learns how agreement or conflict should affect routing rather than imposing a fixed similarity rule; this is useful because discrepancy may reflect either a meaningful local event or noisy evidence depending on diffusion phase. The gate is trained by the generation objective as a conflict-conditioned evidence-reliance coefficient, not as a calibrated probability of evidence correctness. \n486\t\n487\t The meaning of g j g_{j} acts directly in the expert-logit space: the prior and evidence routers first produce logits of the same shape, and g j g_{j} linearly interpolates between them: \n488\t\n489\t ℓ j = ( 1 − g j ) ​ ℓ prior + g j ​ ℓ j evid , 𝐏 j = softmax ⁡ ( ℓ j ) . \\boldsymbol{\\ell}_{j}=(1-g_{j})\\boldsymbol{\\ell}^{\\mathrm{prior}}+g_{j}\\boldsymbol{\\ell}^{\\mathrm{evid}}_{j},\\ \\mathbf{P}_{j}=\\operatorname{softmax}(\\boldsymbol{\\ell}_{j}). \n490\t\n491\t (11) \n492\t\n493\t Thus, g j = 0 g_{j}=0 exactly recovers the prior logits, whereas g j = 1 g_{j}=1 exactly recovers the evidence logits.\n494\tThe top- K K entries of 𝐏 j \\mathbf{P}_{j} select the experts, and their renormalized probabilities directly weight the expert outputs. \n495\t\n496\t Sparse Dispatch and Composition. \n497\t\n498\t Top- K K indices and weights are broadcast to frames within each chunk; active expert outputs are weighted and accumulated, with padding masked out. \n499\t\n500\t The audio output of the layer is an equal-weight combination of the shared expert and the sparse mixture, \n501\t\n502\t 𝐨 aud = 1 2 ​ MLP shared ​ ( 𝐡 aud ) + 1 2 ​ 𝐨 sparse . \\mathbf{o}_{\\text{aud}}=\\tfrac{1}{2}\\,\\mathrm{MLP}_{\\text{shared}}(\\mathbf{h}_{\\text{aud}})+\\tfrac{1}{2}\\,\\mathbf{o}_{\\text{sparse}}. \n503\t\n504\t (12) \n505\t\n506\t The down-projection of each routed expert is zero-initialized, so the sparse contribution starts near zero and is introduced smoothly during optimization. The fixed 1 2 : 1 2 \\tfrac{1}{2}{:}\\tfrac{1}{2} composition prevents an additional learnable mixture gate from suppressing the routed path; gradients can update the expert down-projections from the first step, after which the experts and router co-adapt. The final layer output is [ 𝐨 ctx ∥ 𝐨 aud ] [\\mathbf{o}_{\\text{ctx}}\\;\\|\\;\\mathbf{o}_{\\text{aud}}] . \n507\t\n508\t Load Balancing. \n509\t\n510\t To prevent expert collapse we adopt a Switch-Transformer-style auxiliary loss  [ 10 ] , computed at chunk granularity and only over valid chunks: \n511\t\n512\t ℒ MoE = N e ⋅ ∑ i = 1 N e 𝐩 ¯ i valid ⋅ 𝐝 ¯ i valid , \\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{MoE}}=N_{e}\\cdot\\sum_{i=1}^{N_{e}}\\bar{\\mathbf{p}}_{i}^{\\text{valid}}\\cdot\\bar{\\mathbf{d}}_{i}^{\\text{valid}}, \n513\t\n514\t (13) \n515\t\n516\t where 𝐩 ¯ valid \\bar{\\mathbf{p}}^{\\text{valid}} is the mean router probability over valid chunks and 𝐝 ¯ valid \\bar{\\mathbf{d}}^{\\text{valid}} is the mean top- K K -normalised dispatch fraction. Padded chunks are excluded from both statistics. The layer-wise auxiliary losses are summed across the M M CPE-MoE layers and weighted in the composite objective below. \n517\t\n518\t 3.5 Conditional Flow-Matching Training \n519\t\n520\t Given the ground-truth stereo-VAE latent 𝐱 1 \\mathbf{x}_{1} , we sample 𝐱 0 ∼ 𝒩 ​ ( 0 , I ) \\mathbf{x}_{0}\\!\\sim\\!\\mathcal{N}(0,I) and t ∼ σ ​ ( 𝒩 ​ ( 0 , 1 ) ) t\\!\\sim\\!\\sigma(\\mathcal{N}(0,1)) , form 𝐱 t = ( 1 − t ) ​ 𝐱 0 + t ​ 𝐱 1 \\mathbf{x}_{t}{=}(1{-}t)\\mathbf{x}_{0}{+}t\\mathbf{x}_{1} , and regress the ground-truth velocity: \n521\t\n522\t ℒ FM = 𝔼 ​ ‖ 𝐯 θ ​ ( 𝐱 t , 𝐜 , 𝐜 text , t ) − ( 𝐱 1 − 𝐱 0 ) ‖ 2 . \\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{FM}}=\\mathbb{E}\\big\\|\\mathbf{v}_{\\theta}(\\mathbf{x}_{t},\\mathbf{c},\\mathbf{c}_{\\text{text}},t)-(\\mathbf{x}_{1}-\\mathbf{x}_{0})\\big\\|^{2}. \n523\t\n524\t (14) \n525\t\n526\t During training, we randomly mask a contiguous span of the audio reference and compute the reconstruction loss on the masked region; the audio reference and caption are independently dropped for classifier-free guidance  [ 14 ] . \n527\t\n528\t The composite objective is \n529\t\n530\t ℒ = ℒ FM + α CL ​ ℒ CL + α MoE ​ ℒ MoE . \\mathcal{L}=\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{FM}}+\\alpha_{\\mathrm{CL}}\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{CL}}+\\alpha_{\\mathrm{MoE}}\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{MoE}}. \n531\t\n532\t (15) \n533\t\n534\t 3.6 Inference \n535\t\n536\t We integrate the learned velocity field with a fixed-step Euler ODE solver, using 100 100 uniform steps in t ∈ [ 0 , 1 ] t\\!\\in\\![0,1] ; an optional cosine sway sampling schedule accelerates inference at low step counts. \n537\t\n538\t At inference we adopt Adaptive Projected Guidance (APG)  [ 33 ] and adapt it to the clean-sample estimate induced by the CFM velocity parameterisation. With conditional and unconditional clean-sample estimates μ ^ \\hat{\\mu} and μ ^ ∅ \\hat{\\mu}_{\\varnothing} , let Δ ​ μ = μ ^ − μ ^ ∅ \\Delta\\mu=\\hat{\\mu}-\\hat{\\mu}_{\\varnothing} and decompose it into components parallel and orthogonal to μ ^ \\hat{\\mu} , denoted by Δ ​ μ ∥ \\Delta\\mu_{\\parallel} and Δ ​ μ ⟂ \\Delta\\mu_{\\perp} . The guided estimate is \n539\t\n540\t μ ^ APG = μ ^ ∅ + w ​ ( η ​ Δ ​ μ ∥ + Δ ​ μ ⟂ ) , \\hat{\\mu}_{\\mathrm{APG}}=\\hat{\\mu}_{\\varnothing}+w\\left(\\eta\\,\\Delta\\mu_{\\parallel}+\\Delta\\mu_{\\perp}\\right), \n541\t\n542\t (16) \n543\t\n544\t where w w is the guidance scale and η \\eta damps the parallel component. It is then converted back to velocity space for ODE integration. Momentum smoothing, the clean-sample conversion, and the complete update are given in the appendix. The final clean stereo latent is decoded into waveform audio by the pretrained stereo VAE decoder. \n545\t\n546\t 4 Experiments \n547\t\n548\t 4.1 Experimental Setup \n549\t\n550\t Tasks and Benchmarks. \n551\t\n552\t We evaluate one unified model on three public generation tasks and a controlled complex-scene set. For text-to-speech (TTS), we use SeedTTS-eval  [ 2 ] and LibriSpeech-PC test-clean  [ 27 ] ; for text-to-audio (TTA), we use AudioCaps  [ 19 ] ; for text-to-music (TTM), we use MusicCaps  [ 1 ] and Song Describer  [ 25 ] . The Complex-Scene set is described in the subsequent section. \n553\t\n554\t For SonicWeave and controlled baselines,\n555\twe deterministically render benchmark inputs into a fixed schema using constrained field-extraction rules. Where automatic parsing is needed, a zero-temperature language model acts only as a schema-aware extractor and validator; it is prohibited from introducing information absent from the original input, apart from fixed template defaults applied uniformly across the benchmark. The converter does not observe reference audio. \n556\t\n557\t Baselines. \n558\t\n559\t We compare against representative specialized and general audio generators selected for task coverage, availability, and recency. For Complex-Scene evaluation, we retain Higgs Audio V2, UniFlow-Audio, and Dasheng AudioGen, as they provide the most relevant publicly available coverage of both speech generation and general audio generation for mixed scenes. \n560\t\n561\t Two key ablation models, Dense (1B) and Base-MoE (2B, matching SonicWeave), share SonicWeave’s data and backbone. Dense replaces CPE-MoE with dense FFNs; Base-MoE uses token-level top-K audio routing without conflict-gated prior–evidence fusion.\n562\tThe Dense comparison measures the benefit of conditional capacity, whereas the parameter-matched Base-MoE comparison isolates the effect of the routing design more directly. \n563\t\n564\t The complete model capability comparison is given in Table  1 , while task-specific quantitative comparisons are reported in Tables  2 – 4 . \n565\t\n566\t Metrics. \n567\t\n568\t For all public benchmarks, we report objective metrics suited to each task: WER/CER for TTS; FAD, KL divergence, and CLAP for TTA; and KL divergence and CLAP for TTM. The TTA and TTM KL/CLAP scores follow the Stable Audio metrics protocol with task-specific checkpoints, while TTA FAD uses the AVBench FD VGG implementation. For the Complex-Scene evaluation, we use Gemini-based reference-free judging together with human listening tests.\n569\tThe two are used jointly because the reference-free judge provides scalable coverage over all prompts, while human MOS offers an independent perceptual assessment on a fixed subset.\n570\tGemini assigns 1-5 scores for semantic adherence and technical quality from the same prompt; the concise scoring rubric is given in the appendix. \n571\t\n572\t Implementation Details. \n573\t\n574\t The CPE-MoE module replaces the dense FFN in the last 4 4 layers, with 4 4 routed experts and top- 2 2 selection at chunk size C = 4 C{=}4 . Inference uses a fixed-step Euler ODE solver with APG guidance. Training uses roughly five million 10–15-second clips sampled from an internal corpus exceeding 20,000 hours, in which speech, music, sound effects, ambience, and mixed recordings coexist in a shared data distribution. Additional model and optimization details, together with targeted studies of gate adaptivity and chunk-size sensitivity, are provided in the appendix. \n575\t\n576\t 4.2 Results on Public Benchmarks \n577\t\n578\t Tables  2 - 4 summarize the public-benchmark results. On TTS, SonicWeave achieves the best or tied-best result on all three subsets, tying the strongest reported results on SeedTTS-eval and LibriSpeech. Relative to the controlled baselines, it improves over Dense by 1.4, 1.0, and 2.5 percentage points on these subsets, and over Base-MoE by 0.4, 0.3, and 0.8 points, respectively. \n579\t\n580\t Table 2: TTS results on SeedTTS and LibriSpeech. \n581\t\n582\t Model \n583\t SeedTTS-en \n584\t SeedTTS-zh \n585\t LibriSpeech \n586\t\n587\t WER ↓ \\downarrow \n588\t\n589\t CER ↓ \\downarrow \n590\t\n591\t WER ↓ \\downarrow \n592\t\n593\t F5-TTS \n594\t 1.9% \n595\t 1.6% \n596\t 2.4% \n597\t\n598\t CosyVoice 2 \n599\t 2.0% \n600\t 1.5% \n601\t 2.5% \n602\t\n603\t UniAudio \n604\t 7.2% \n605\t – \n606\t 18.3% \n607\t\n608\t Qwen2.5-Omni \n609\t 2.1% \n610\t 1.6% \n611\t 7.6% \n612\t\n613\t UniMoE-Audio \n614\t 1.3% \n615\t 0.8% \n616\t 3.4% \n617\t\n618\t Higgs Audio V2 \n619\t 1.0% \n620\t 0.8% \n621\t 3.6% \n622\t\n623\t Step-Audio \n624\t 2.2% \n625\t 1.0% \n626\t 5.0% \n627\t\n628\t Dense (ours) \n629\t 2.4% \n630\t 1.8% \n631\t 4.9% \n632\t\n633\t Base-MoE (ours) \n634\t 1.4% \n635\t 1.1% \n636\t 3.2% \n637\t\n638\t SonicWeave (ours) \n639\t 1.0% \n640\t 0.8% \n641\t 2.4% \n642\t\n643\t On TTA, SonicWeave substantially improves over Dense and Base-MoE in all three metrics. Its CLAP score reaches 0.475, close to the strongest reported baseline (0.480), while its KL is 1.26 and its FAD is 2.75.\n644\tSonicWeave does not obtain the best value on every individual AudioCaps metric, but it narrows the gap to the strongest CLAP system while substantially improving FAD and KL over the matched controls. \n645\t\n646\t Table 3: TTA results on AudioCaps. \n647\t\n648\t Model \n649\t FAD ↓ \\downarrow \n650\t\n651\t KL ↓ \\downarrow \n652\t\n653\t CLAP ↑ \\uparrow \n654\t\n655\t AudioLDM 2 \n656\t 2.29 \n657\t 1.41 \n658\t 0.419 \n659\t\n660\t TangoFlux \n661\t 2.26 \n662\t 1.15 \n663\t 0.480 \n664\t\n665\t Stable Audio Open \n666\t 4.13 \n667\t 2.14 \n668\t 0.350 \n669\t\n670\t Dasheng AudioGen \n671\t 3.19 \n672\t 1.86 \n673\t 0.438 \n674\t\n675\t UniAudio \n676\t 6.64 \n677\t – \n678\t 0.243 \n679\t\n680\t UniFlow-Audio \n681\t 5.74 \n682\t 1.43 \n683\t 0.476 \n684\t\n685\t AudioX \n686\t 2.45 \n687\t 1.27 \n688\t 0.440 \n689\t\n690\t Dense (ours) \n691\t 3.98 \n692\t 1.62 \n693\t 0.389 \n694\t\n695\t Base-MoE (ours) \n696\t 3.10 \n697\t 1.40 \n698\t 0.422 \n699\t\n700\t SonicWeave (ours) \n701\t 2.75 \n702\t 1.26 \n703\t 0.475 \n704\t\n705\t On TTM, SonicWeave achieves the lowest KL and highest CLAP on Song Describer among the listed systems.\n706\tOn MusicCaps, it achieves a competitive KL of 1.06 and CLAP of 0.312, offering a strong quality–alignment trade-off and placing it on the Pareto frontier among the listed baselines.\n707\tCompared with Dense and Base-MoE, SonicWeave consistently improves both KL and CLAP across the two datasets, showing that CPE-MoE strengthens text–music correspondence without uniformly surpassing every specialized baseline.\n708\t\n709\t Table 4: TTM results on MusicCaps and Song Describer. \n710\t\n711\t Model \n712\t MusicCaps \n713\t Song Describer \n714\t\n715\t CLAP ↑ \\uparrow \n716\t\n717\t KL ↓ \\downarrow \n718\t\n719\t CLAP ↑ \\uparrow \n720\t\n721\t KL ↓ \\downarrow \n722\t\n723\t AudioLDM 2 \n724\t 0.301 \n725\t 1.20 \n726\t – \n727\t – \n728\t\n729\t MusicGen-Large \n730\t 0.280 \n731\t 1.31 \n732\t 0.19 \n733\t 0.54 \n734\t\n735\t Stable Audio Open \n736\t 0.300 \n737\t 1.44 \n738\t 0.32 \n739\t 0.62 \n740\t\n741\t AudioX \n742\t 0.240 \n743\t 0.96 \n744\t – \n745\t – \n746\t\n747\t Dasheng AudioGen \n748\t 0.334 \n749\t 1.37 \n750\t – \n751\t – \n752\t\n753\t UniMoE-Audio \n754\t 0.340 \n755\t 1.25 \n756\t 0.22 \n757\t – \n758\t\n759\t UniFlow-Audio \n760\t 0.241 \n761\t 1.87 \n762\t 0.15 \n763\t – \n764\t\n765\t Dense (ours) \n766\t 0.251 \n767\t 1.31 \n768\t 0.29 \n769\t 0.58 \n770\t\n771\t Base-MoE (ours) \n772\t 0.296 \n773\t 1.10 \n774\t 0.32 \n775\t 0.48 \n776\t\n777\t SonicWeave (ours) \n778\t 0.312 \n779\t 1.06 \n780\t 0.36 \n781\t 0.44 \n782\t\n783\t 4.3 Complex-Scene Evaluation \n784\t\n785\t (a) (b) \n786\t\n787\t Figure 3: Routing analysis on the balanced routing benchmark. (a) Content-conditioned expert dispatch across routed layers, showing non-uniform and content-dependent specialization. (b) Mean conflict gate 𝔼 ​ [ g j | t ] \\mathbb{E}[g_{j}|t] across diffusion phases, showing content-dependent and phase-dependent prior–evidence balancing. \n788\t\n789\t Public benchmarks mainly evaluate isolated speech, music, or environmental sounds and therefore underrepresent the heterogeneous compositions targeted by SonicWeave. We construct a controlled suite of 100 manually curated complex-scene prompts, comprising 45 speech-with-background cases, 10 singing-with-background cases, 15 clean dialogues, and 30 dialogues with background.\n790\tEach case is provided in both natural-language and structured-caption forms.\n791\tPublic systems receive the natural-language prompt in their native input format, whereas SonicWeave, Dense and Base-MoE receive the corresponding structured caption containing the same\n792\tsemantic specifications.\n793\t\n794\t Table 5: Reference-free and human listening evaluation on the Complex-Scene suite. AI-Tech and AI-Sem denote technical quality and semantic adherence, respectively; MOS-Q measures perceived audio quality, while MOS-R measures whether the requested prompt events are jointly realized. \n795\t\n796\t Model \n797\t AI-Tech ↑ \\uparrow \n798\t\n799\t AI-Sem ↑ \\uparrow \n800\t\n801\t MOS-Q ↑ \\uparrow \n802\t\n803\t MOS-R ↑ \\uparrow \n804\t\n805\t Higgs Audio V2 \n806\t 4.32 \n807\t 3.54 \n808\t 3.92 ±0.21 \n809\t\n810\t 2.42 ±0.64 \n811\t\n812\t UniFlow-Audio \n813\t 2.28 \n814\t 1.77 \n815\t 1.99 ±0.33 \n816\t\n817\t 1.87 ±0.80 \n818\t\n819\t Dasheng AudioGen \n820\t 3.10 \n821\t 3.07 \n822\t 2.97 ±0.41 \n823\t\n824\t 3.25 ±0.73 \n825\t\n826\t Dense (ours) \n827\t 4.13 \n828\t 4.23 \n829\t 4.29 ±0.24 \n830\t\n831\t 4.15 ±0.37 \n832\t\n833\t Base-MoE (ours) \n834\t 4.62 \n835\t 4.58 \n836\t 4.57 ±0.13 \n837\t 4.31 ±0.31 \n838\t\n839\t SonicWeave (ours) \n840\t 4.79 \n841\t 4.72 \n842\t 4.57 ±0.19 \n843\t 4.49 ±0.38 \n844\t\n845\t Table  5 shows consistent trends between the reference-free AI evaluation and human listening tests.\n846\tSonicWeave achieves the highest AI semantic-adherence and technical-quality scores and the highest human MOS-R. Higgs Audio V2 retains relatively competitive perceived quality but receives a much lower request-realization score, showing that audio quality alone does not imply faithful realization of all requested scene components.\n847\tDasheng AudioGen and UniFlow-Audio additionally show less consistent performance across the heterogeneous prompts.\n848\tSonicWeave and Base-MoE are effectively tied on MOS-Q, whereas SonicWeave is consistently stronger in semantic adherence and request realization. This supports the intended role of CPE-MoE: it preserves the acoustic quality of the underlying generator while improving compositional fidelity and fine-grained control in complex scenes.\n849\t\n850\t 4.4 Routing Analysis and Visualization \n851\t\n852\t To explain the behavior of SonicWeave beyond aggregate generation metrics, we analyze the routing patterns induced by its CPE-MoE module. The main-text visualization focuses on population-level evidence: content-conditioned expert usage and the evolution of the conflict gate across diffusion phases. Representative mixed-scene traces are reserved for the appendix because they illustrate the mechanism qualitatively rather than providing a statistically aggregated result. \n853\t\n854\t For this analysis, we construct a 250-prompt routing benchmark by sampling examples from several single-task evaluation sets and combining them with complex-scene prompts. We group the resulting prompts into speech, music, singing, SFX, and mixed-scene categories, and aggregate routing statistics over all valid chunks and routed layers. \n855\t\n856\t Content-conditioned expert specialization. \n857\t\n858\t Figure  3 (a) shows the mean dispatch fraction for each expert across the four CPE-MoE layers and five content groups. The load is clearly non-uniform and content dependent: speech and SFX favor different expert combinations from music and singing, while mixed scenes retain a distinct distribution rather than collapsing to one of the single-content patterns. This specialization is also layer dependent.\n859\tFor example, music and singing place more mass on different experts in the deeper routed layers, whereas speech consistently emphasizes a smaller subset of experts.\n860\tThese observations indicate that the MoE is using conditional capacity to form acoustic specializations, rather than merely distributing tokens uniformly across experts. \n861\t\n862\t Phase-dependent conflict gating. \n863\t\n864\t Figure  3 (b) reports 𝔼 ​ [ g j | t ] \\mathbb{E}[g_{j}|t] for each content group. The curves are content dependent and non-monotonic: the gate changes rapidly during early structure formation, reaches content-specific operating ranges, and gradually returns toward a more balanced regime during late acoustic refinement. Speech and SFX maintain higher evidence reliance than music and singing over much of the trajectory, while mixed scenes lie between these regimes. Thus, the model does not apply a single fixed prior–evidence interpolation to all audio. Instead, it adjusts evidence reliance as a function of both acoustic content and diffusion phase, consistent with the intended role of the conflict gate. \n865\t\n866\t 5 Conclusion \n867\t\n868\t This paper presented SonicWeave, a unified flow-matching model for generating speech, singing, music, sound effects, and their mixtures with a single set of parameters. The central premise of this work is that conditional computation in unified audio must operate at two coupled scales: it must accommodate differences across acoustic domains while adapting to heterogeneous regions within the same scene. CPE-MoE addresses this requirement by routing temporally contiguous audio chunks through a learned combination of a global text-and-diffusion prior and local acoustic evidence, while preserving a shared conditioning pathway for text and time tokens. \n869\t\n870\t Across public TTS, text-to-audio, and text-to-music benchmarks, SonicWeave consistently improves over matched Dense and token-routed MoE controls. On the Complex-Scene suite, the principal gain is compositional: SonicWeave improves semantic adherence and joint request realization while maintaining perceptual quality comparable to the parameter-matched Base-MoE.\n871\tQuantitatively, it reduces TTA FAD by 30.9% over Dense and improves Complex-Scene MOS-R by 0.18 over Base-MoE and by more than 1.2 points over the strongest external baseline.\n872\tThe learned expert assignments and conflict-gate trajectories also vary with content, layer depth, and diffusion phase, providing evidence that the additional capacity is used conditionally rather than uniformly. \n873\t\n874\t Targeted studies reported in the appendix further compare the learned gate with fixed prior–evidence averaging and vary the routing chunk size. Fixed fusion degrades all reported task groups, while increasing the chunk size from 4 to 8 weakens most metrics, supporting adaptive fusion and relatively fine-grained chunk routing. \n875\t\n876\t These findings support chunk-level prior–evidence routing as a useful intermediate granularity for unified audio generation. At the same time, the current evaluation is limited to short-form generation, a two-speaker structured interface, and non-exhaustive routing ablations. Extending the approach to longer scenes, richer speaker interactions, and explicit temporal plans remains an important direction. \n877\t\n878\t References \n879\t\n880\t [1] \n881\t A. Agostinelli, T. I. Denk, Z. Borsos, J. H. Engel, M. Verzetti, A. Caillon, Q. Huang, A. Jansen, A. Roberts, M. Tagliasacchi, M. Sharifi, N. Zeghidour, and C. H. Frank (2023) \n882\t\n883\t MusicLM: generating music from text .\n884\t\n885\t CoRR abs/2301.11325 .\n886\t\n887\t External Links: Document \n888\t\n889\t Cited by: §4.1 .\n890\t\n891\t [2] \n892\t P. Anastassiou, J. Chen, J. Chen, Y. Chen, Z. Chen, Z. Chen, J. Cong, L. Deng, C. Ding, L. Gao, M. Gong, P. Huang, Q. Huang, Z. Huang, Y. Huo, D. Jia, C. Li, F. Li, H. Li, J. Li, X. Li, X. Li, L. Liu, S. Liu, S. Liu, X. Liu, Y. Liu, Z. Liu, L. Lu, J. Pan, X. Wang, Y. Wang, Y. Wang, Z. Wei, J. Wu, C. Yao, Y. Yang, Y. Yi, J. Zhang, Q. Zhang, S. Zhang, W. Zhang, Y. Zhang, Z. Zhao, D. Zhong, and X. Zhuang (2024) \n893\t\n894\t Seed-TTS: a family of high-quality versatile speech generation models .\n895\t\n896\t CoRR abs/2406.02430 .\n897\t\n898\t External Links: Document \n899\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6b970116-bc68-429c-9d83-8e23452428b2","uuid":"f64f7be1-8ac9-49fb-a21e-be8a893c6648","timestamp":"2026-08-11T06:47:23.168Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/sonicweave.txt","content":"\n ✓ \\checkmark \n ✓ \\checkmark \n ✓ \\checkmark \n △ \\triangle \n – \n – \n – \n\n AudioX  [ 35 ] \n\n – \n ✓ \\checkmark \n ✓ \\checkmark \n – \n – \n – \n – \n\n Higgs Audio V2  [ 4 ] \n\n ✓ \\checkmark \n ✓ \\checkmark \n △ \\triangle \n △ \\triangle \n – \n ✓ \\checkmark \n △ \\triangle \n\n UniMoE-Audio  [ 24 ] \n\n ✓ \\checkmark \n – \n ✓ \\checkmark \n – \n – \n – \n – \n\n UniFlow-Audio  [ 40 ] \n\n ✓ \\checkmark \n ✓ \\checkmark \n ✓ \\checkmark \n △ \\triangle \n – \n △ \\triangle \n △ \\triangle \n\n Dasheng AudioGen  [ 26 ] \n\n ✓ \\checkmark \n ✓ \\checkmark \n ✓ \\checkmark \n ✓ \\checkmark \n – \n △ \\triangle \n △ \\triangle \n\n General / compositional \n SonicWeave (ours) \n ✓ \\checkmark \n ✓ \\checkmark \n ✓ \\checkmark \n ✓ \\checkmark \n ✓ \\checkmark \n ✓ \\checkmark \n ✓ \\checkmark \n\n The prior gives a global expert preference, but local audio becomes informative at different rates across diffusion phases and acoustic regions. We therefore let each chunk decide how far its routing should move from the prior toward the local evidence. We first project the semantic–phase summary into the d d -dimensional hidden space of the joint DiT: \n\n 𝐩 ~ = W align ​ 𝐩 ∈ ℝ d . \\tilde{\\mathbf{p}}=W_{\\mathrm{align}}\\mathbf{p}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}. \n\n (8) \n\n The gate observes both signals and their explicit pairwise interactions: \n\n ϕ j = LN ( [ 𝐩 ~ ∥ 𝐞 j ∥ 𝐩 ~ ⊙ 𝐞 j ∥ | 𝐩 ~ − 𝐞 j | ] ) , \\boldsymbol{\\phi}_{j}=\\mathrm{LN}\\!\\left([\\,\\tilde{\\mathbf{p}}\\;\\|\\;\\mathbf{e}_{j}\\;\\|\\;\\tilde{\\mathbf{p}}\\!\\odot\\!\\mathbf{e}_{j}\\;\\|\\;|\\tilde{\\mathbf{p}}-\\mathbf{e}_{j}|\\,]\\right), \n\n (9) \n\n g j = σ ​ ( MLP g ⁡ ( ϕ j ) ) . g_{j}=\\sigma\\!\\left(\\operatorname{MLP}_{g}(\\boldsymbol{\\phi}_{j})\\right). \n\n (10) \n\n Following pairwise matching features [ 6 ] , the product exposes coordinate-wise agreement and the absolute difference exposes disagreement, while the original vectors retain source-specific information. The MLP therefore learns how agreement or conflict should affect routing rather than imposing a fixed similarity rule; this is useful because discrepancy may reflect either a meaningful local event or noisy evidence depending on diffusion phase. The gate is trained by the generation objective as a conflict-conditioned evidence-reliance coefficient, not as a calibrated probability of evidence correctness. \n\n The meaning of g j g_{j} acts directly in the expert-logit space: the prior and evidence routers first produce logits of the same shape, and g j g_{j} linearly interpolates between them: \n\n ℓ j = ( 1 − g j ) ​ ℓ prior + g j ​ ℓ j evid , 𝐏 j = softmax ⁡ ( ℓ j ) . \\boldsymbol{\\ell}_{j}=(1-g_{j})\\boldsymbol{\\ell}^{\\mathrm{prior}}+g_{j}\\boldsymbol{\\ell}^{\\mathrm{evid}}_{j},\\ \\mathbf{P}_{j}=\\operatorname{softmax}(\\boldsymbol{\\ell}_{j}). \n\n (11) \n\n Thus, g j = 0 g_{j}=0 exactly recovers the prior logits, whereas g j = 1 g_{j}=1 exactly recovers the evidence logits.\nThe top- K K entries of 𝐏 j \\mathbf{P}_{j} select the experts, and their renormalized probabilities directly weight the expert outputs. \n\n Sparse Dispatch and Composition. \n\n Top- K K indices and weights are broadcast to frames within each chunk; active expert outputs are weighted and accumulated, with padding masked out. \n\n The audio output of the layer is an equal-weight combination of the shared expert and the sparse mixture, \n\n 𝐨 aud = 1 2 ​ MLP shared ​ ( 𝐡 aud ) + 1 2 ​ 𝐨 sparse . \\mathbf{o}_{\\text{aud}}=\\tfrac{1}{2}\\,\\mathrm{MLP}_{\\text{shared}}(\\mathbf{h}_{\\text{aud}})+\\tfrac{1}{2}\\,\\mathbf{o}_{\\text{sparse}}. \n\n (12) \n\n The down-projection of each routed expert is zero-initialized, so the sparse contribution starts near zero and is introduced smoothly during optimization. The fixed 1 2 : 1 2 \\tfrac{1}{2}{:}\\tfrac{1}{2} composition prevents an additional learnable mixture gate from suppressing the routed path; gradients can update the expert down-projections from the first step, after which the experts and router co-adapt. The final layer output is [ 𝐨 ctx ∥ 𝐨 aud ] [\\mathbf{o}_{\\text{ctx}}\\;\\|\\;\\mathbf{o}_{\\text{aud}}] . \n\n Load Balancing. \n\n To prevent expert collapse we adopt a Switch-Transformer-style auxiliary loss  [ 10 ] , computed at chunk granularity and only over valid chunks: \n\n ℒ MoE = N e ⋅ ∑ i = 1 N e 𝐩 ¯ i valid ⋅ 𝐝 ¯ i valid , \\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{MoE}}=N_{e}\\cdot\\sum_{i=1}^{N_{e}}\\bar{\\mathbf{p}}_{i}^{\\text{valid}}\\cdot\\bar{\\mathbf{d}}_{i}^{\\text{valid}}, \n\n (13) \n\n where 𝐩 ¯ valid \\bar{\\mathbf{p}}^{\\text{valid}} is the mean router probability over valid chunks and 𝐝 ¯ valid \\bar{\\mathbf{d}}^{\\text{valid}} is the mean top- K K -normalised dispatch fraction. Padded chunks are excluded from both statistics. The layer-wise auxiliary losses are summed across the M M CPE-MoE layers and weighted in the composite objective below. \n\n 3.5 Conditional Flow-Matching Training \n\n Given the ground-truth stereo-VAE latent 𝐱 1 \\mathbf{x}_{1} , we sample 𝐱 0 ∼ 𝒩 ​ ( 0 , I ) \\mathbf{x}_{0}\\!\\sim\\!\\mathcal{N}(0,I) and t ∼ σ ​ ( 𝒩 ​ ( 0 , 1 ) ) t\\!\\sim\\!\\sigma(\\mathcal{N}(0,1)) , form 𝐱 t = ( 1 − t ) ​ 𝐱 0 + t ​ 𝐱 1 \\mathbf{x}_{t}{=}(1{-}t)\\mathbf{x}_{0}{+}t\\mathbf{x}_{1} , and regress the ground-truth velocity: \n\n ℒ FM = 𝔼 ​ ‖ 𝐯 θ ​ ( 𝐱 t , 𝐜 , 𝐜 text , t ) − ( 𝐱 1 − 𝐱 0 ) ‖ 2 . \\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{FM}}=\\mathbb{E}\\big\\|\\mathbf{v}_{\\theta}(\\mathbf{x}_{t},\\mathbf{c},\\mathbf{c}_{\\text{text}},t)-(\\mathbf{x}_{1}-\\mathbf{x}_{0})\\big\\|^{2}. \n\n (14) \n\n During training, we randomly mask a contiguous span of the audio reference and compute the reconstruction loss on the masked region; the audio reference and caption are independently dropped for classifier-free guidance  [ 14 ] . \n\n The composite objective is \n\n ℒ = ℒ FM + α CL ​ ℒ CL + α MoE ​ ℒ MoE . \\mathcal{L}=\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{FM}}+\\alpha_{\\mathrm{CL}}\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{CL}}+\\alpha_{\\mathrm{MoE}}\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{MoE}}. \n\n (15) \n\n 3.6 Inference \n\n We integrate the learned velocity field with a fixed-step Euler ODE solver, using 100 100 uniform steps in t ∈ [ 0 , 1 ] t\\!\\in\\![0,1] ; an optional cosine sway sampling schedule accelerates inference at low step counts. \n\n At inference we adopt Adaptive Projected Guidance (APG)  [ 33 ] and adapt it to the clean-sample estimate induced by the CFM velocity parameterisation. With conditional and unconditional clean-sample estimates μ ^ \\hat{\\mu} and μ ^ ∅ \\hat{\\mu}_{\\varnothing} , let Δ ​ μ = μ ^ − μ ^ ∅ \\Delta\\mu=\\hat{\\mu}-\\hat{\\mu}_{\\varnothing} and decompose it into components parallel and orthogonal to μ ^ \\hat{\\mu} , denoted by Δ ​ μ ∥ \\Delta\\mu_{\\parallel} and Δ ​ μ ⟂ \\Delta\\mu_{\\perp} . The guided estimate is \n\n μ ^ APG = μ ^ ∅ + w ​ ( η ​ Δ ​ μ ∥ + Δ ​ μ ⟂ ) , \\hat{\\mu}_{\\mathrm{APG}}=\\hat{\\mu}_{\\varnothing}+w\\left(\\eta\\,\\Delta\\mu_{\\parallel}+\\Delta\\mu_{\\perp}\\right), \n\n (16) \n\n where w w is the guidance scale and η \\eta damps the parallel component. It is then converted back to velocity space for ODE integration. Momentum smoothing, the clean-sample conversion, and the complete update are given in the appendix. The final clean stereo latent is decoded into waveform audio by the pretrained stereo VAE decoder. \n\n 4 Experiments \n\n 4.1 Experimental Setup \n\n Tasks and Benchmarks. \n\n We evaluate one unified model on three public generation tasks and a controlled complex-scene set. For text-to-speech (TTS), we use SeedTTS-eval  [ 2 ] and LibriSpeech-PC test-clean  [ 27 ] ; for text-to-audio (TTA), we use AudioCaps  [ 19 ] ; for text-to-music (TTM), we use MusicCaps  [ 1 ] and Song Describer  [ 25 ] . The Complex-Scene set is described in the subsequent section. \n\n For SonicWeave and controlled baselines,\nwe deterministically render benchmark inputs into a fixed schema using constrained field-extraction rules. Where automatic parsing is needed, a zero-temperature language model acts only as a schema-aware extractor and validator; it is prohibited from introducing information absent from the original input, apart from fixed template defaults applied uniformly across the benchmark. The converter does not observe reference audio. \n\n Baselines. \n\n We compare against representative specialized and general audio generators selected for task coverage, availability, and recency. For Complex-Scene evaluation, we retain Higgs Audio V2, UniFlow-Audio, and Dasheng AudioGen, as they provide the most relevant publicly available coverage of both speech generation and general audio generation for mixed scenes. \n\n Two key ablation models, Dense (1B) and Base-MoE (2B, matching SonicWeave), share SonicWeave’s data and backbone. Dense replaces CPE-MoE with dense FFNs; Base-MoE uses token-level top-K audio routing without conflict-gated prior–evidence fusion.\nThe Dense comparison measures the benefit of conditional capacity, whereas the parameter-matched Base-MoE comparison isolates the effect of the routing design more directly. \n\n The complete model capability comparison is given in Table  1 , while task-specific quantitative comparisons are reported in Tables  2 – 4 . \n\n Metrics. \n\n For all public benchmarks, we report objective metrics suited to each task: WER/CER for TTS; FAD, KL divergence, and CLAP for TTA; and KL divergence and CLAP for TTM. The TTA and TTM KL/CLAP scores follow the Stable Audio metrics protocol with task-specific checkpoints, while TTA FAD uses the AVBench FD VGG implementation. For the Complex-Scene evaluation, we use Gemini-based reference-free judging together with human listening tests.\nThe two are used jointly because the reference-free judge provides scalable coverage over all prompts, while human MOS offers an independent perceptual assessment on a fixed subset.\nGemini assigns 1-5 scores for semantic adherence and technical quality from the same prompt; the concise scoring rubric is given in the appendix. \n\n Implementation Details. \n\n The CPE-MoE module replaces the dense FFN in the last 4 4 layers, with 4 4 routed experts and top- 2 2 selection at chunk size C = 4 C{=}4 . Inference uses a fixed-step Euler ODE solver with APG guidance. Training uses roughly five million 10–15-second clips sampled from an internal corpus exceeding 20,000 hours, in which speech, music, sound effects, ambience, and mixed recordings coexist in a shared data distribution. Additional model and optimization details, together with targeted studies of gate adaptivity and chunk-size sensitivity, are provided in the appendix. \n\n 4.2 Results on Public Benchmarks \n\n Tables  2 - 4 summarize the public-benchmark results. On TTS, SonicWeave achieves the best or tied-best result on all three subsets, tying the strongest reported results on SeedTTS-eval and LibriSpeech. Relative to the controlled baselines, it improves over Dense by 1.4, 1.0, and 2.5 percentage points on these subsets, and over Base-MoE by 0.4, 0.3, and 0.8 points, respectively. \n\n Table 2: TTS results on SeedTTS and LibriSpeech. \n\n Model \n SeedTTS-en \n SeedTTS-zh \n LibriSpeech \n\n WER ↓ \\downarrow \n\n CER ↓ \\downarrow \n\n WER ↓ \\downarrow \n\n F5-TTS \n 1.9% \n 1.6% \n 2.4% \n\n CosyVoice 2 \n 2.0% \n 1.5% \n 2.5% \n\n UniAudio \n 7.2% \n – \n 18.3% \n\n Qwen2.5-Omni \n 2.1% \n 1.6% \n 7.6% \n\n UniMoE-Audio \n 1.3% \n 0.8% \n 3.4% \n\n Higgs Audio V2 \n 1.0% \n 0.8% \n 3.6% \n\n Step-Audio \n 2.2% \n 1.0% \n 5.0% \n\n Dense (ours) \n 2.4% \n 1.8% \n 4.9% \n\n Base-MoE (ours) \n 1.4% \n 1.1% \n 3.2% \n\n SonicWeave (ours) \n 1.0% \n 0.8% \n 2.4% \n\n On TTA, SonicWeave substantially improves over Dense and Base-MoE in all three metrics. Its CLAP score reaches 0.475, close to the strongest reported baseline (0.480), while its KL is 1.26 and its FAD is 2.75.\nSonicWeave does not obtain the best value on every individual AudioCaps metric, but it narrows the gap to the strongest CLAP system while substantially improving FAD and KL over the matched controls. \n\n Table 3: TTA results on AudioCaps. \n\n Model \n FAD ↓ \\downarrow \n\n KL ↓ \\downarrow \n\n CLAP ↑ \\uparrow \n\n AudioLDM 2 \n 2.29 \n 1.41 \n 0.419 \n\n TangoFlux \n 2.26 \n 1.15 \n 0.480 \n\n Stable Audio Open \n 4.13 \n 2.14 \n 0.350 \n\n Dasheng AudioGen \n 3.19 \n 1.86 \n 0.438 \n\n UniAudio \n 6.64 \n – \n 0.243 \n\n UniFlow-Audio \n 5.74 \n 1.43 \n 0.476 \n\n AudioX \n 2.45 \n 1.27 \n 0.440 \n\n Dense (ours) \n 3.98 \n 1.62 \n 0.389 \n\n Base-MoE (ours) \n 3.10 \n 1.40 \n 0.422 \n\n SonicWeave (ours) \n 2.75 \n 1.26 \n 0.475 \n\n On TTM, SonicWeave achieves the lowest KL and highest CLAP on Song Describer among the listed systems.\nOn MusicCaps, it achieves a competitive KL of 1.06 and CLAP of 0.312, offering a strong quality–alignment trade-off and placing it on the Pareto frontier among the listed baselines.\nCompared with Dense and Base-MoE, SonicWeave consistently improves both KL and CLAP across the two datasets, showing that CPE-MoE strengthens text–music correspondence without uniformly surpassing every specialized baseline.\n\n Table 4: TTM results on MusicCaps and Song Describer. \n\n Model \n MusicCaps \n Song Describer \n\n CLAP ↑ \\uparrow \n\n KL ↓ \\downarrow \n\n CLAP ↑ \\uparrow \n\n KL ↓ \\downarrow \n\n AudioLDM 2 \n 0.301 \n 1.20 \n – \n – \n\n MusicGen-Large \n 0.280 \n 1.31 \n 0.19 \n 0.54 \n\n Stable Audio Open \n 0.300 \n 1.44 \n 0.32 \n 0.62 \n\n AudioX \n 0.240 \n 0.96 \n – \n – \n\n Dasheng AudioGen \n 0.334 \n 1.37 \n – \n – \n\n UniMoE-Audio \n 0.340 \n 1.25 \n 0.22 \n – \n\n UniFlow-Audio \n 0.241 \n 1.87 \n 0.15 \n – \n\n Dense (ours) \n 0.251 \n 1.31 \n 0.29 \n 0.58 \n\n Base-MoE (ours) \n 0.296 \n 1.10 \n 0.32 \n 0.48 \n\n SonicWeave (ours) \n 0.312 \n 1.06 \n 0.36 \n 0.44 \n\n 4.3 Complex-Scene Evaluation \n\n (a) (b) \n\n Figure 3: Routing analysis on the balanced routing benchmark. (a) Content-conditioned expert dispatch across routed layers, showing non-uniform and content-dependent specialization. (b) Mean conflict gate 𝔼 ​ [ g j | t ] \\mathbb{E}[g_{j}|t] across diffusion phases, showing content-dependent and phase-dependent prior–evidence balancing. \n\n Public benchmarks mainly evaluate isolated speech, music, or environmental sounds and therefore underrepresent the heterogeneous compositions targeted by SonicWeave. We construct a controlled suite of 100 manually curated complex-scene prompts, comprising 45 speech-with-background cases, 10 singing-with-background cases, 15 clean dialogues, and 30 dialogues with background.\nEach case is provided in both natural-language and structured-caption forms.\nPublic systems receive the natural-language prompt in their native input format, whereas SonicWeave, Dense and Base-MoE receive the corresponding structured caption containing the same\nsemantic specifications.\n\n Table 5: Reference-free and human listening evaluation on the Complex-Scene suite. AI-Tech and AI-Sem denote technical quality and semantic adherence, respectively; MOS-Q measures perceived audio quality, while MOS-R measures whether the requested prompt events are jointly realized. \n\n Model \n AI-Tech ↑ \\uparrow \n\n AI-Sem ↑ \\uparrow \n\n MOS-Q ↑ \\uparrow \n\n MOS-R ↑ \\uparrow \n\n Higgs Audio V2 \n 4.32 \n 3.54 \n 3.92 ±0.21 \n\n 2.42 ±0.64 \n\n UniFlow-Audio \n 2.28 \n 1.77 \n 1.99 ±0.33 \n\n 1.87 ±0.80 \n\n Dasheng AudioGen \n 3.10 \n 3.07 \n 2.97 ±0.41 \n\n 3.25 ±0.73 \n\n Dense (ours) \n 4.13 \n 4.23 \n 4.29 ±0.24 \n\n 4.15 ±0.37 \n\n Base-MoE (ours) \n 4.62 \n 4.58 \n 4.57 ±0.13 \n 4.31 ±0.31 \n\n SonicWeave (ours) \n 4.79 \n 4.72 \n 4.57 ±0.19 \n 4.49 ±0.38 \n\n Table  5 shows consistent trends between the reference-free AI evaluation and human listening tests.\nSonicWeave achieves the highest AI semantic-adherence and technical-quality scores and the highest human MOS-R. Higgs Audio V2 retains relatively competitive perceived quality but receives a much lower request-realization score, showing that audio quality alone does not imply faithful realization of all requested scene components.\nDasheng AudioGen and UniFlow-Audio additionally show less consistent performance across the heterogeneous prompts.\nSonicWeave and Base-MoE are effectively tied on MOS-Q, whereas SonicWeave is consistently stronger in semantic adherence and request realization. This supports the intended role of CPE-MoE: it preserves the acoustic quality of the underlying generator while improving compositional fidelity and fine-grained control in complex scenes.\n\n 4.4 Routing Analysis and Visualization \n\n To explain the behavior of SonicWeave beyond aggregate generation metrics, we analyze the routing patterns induced by its CPE-MoE module. The main-text visualization focuses on population-level evidence: content-conditioned expert usage and the evolution of the conflict gate across diffusion phases. Representative mixed-scene traces are reserved for the appendix because they illustrate the mechanism qualitatively rather than providing a statistically aggregated result. \n\n For this analysis, we construct a 250-prompt routing benchmark by sampling examples from several single-task evaluation sets and combining them with complex-scene prompts. We group the resulting prompts into speech, music, singing, SFX, and mixed-scene categories, and aggregate routing statistics over all valid chunks and routed layers. \n\n Content-conditioned expert specialization. \n\n Figure  3 (a) shows the mean dispatch fraction for each expert across the four CPE-MoE layers and five content groups. The load is clearly non-uniform and content dependent: speech and SFX favor different expert combinations from music and singing, while mixed scenes retain a distinct distribution rather than collapsing to one of the single-content patterns. This specialization is also layer dependent.\nFor example, music and singing place more mass on different experts in the deeper routed layers, whereas speech consistently emphasizes a smaller subset of experts.\nThese observations indicate that the MoE is using conditional capacity to form acoustic specializations, rather than merely distributing tokens uniformly across experts. \n\n Phase-dependent conflict gating. \n\n Figure  3 (b) reports 𝔼 ​ [ g j | t ] \\mathbb{E}[g_{j}|t] for each content group. The curves are content dependent and non-monotonic: the gate changes rapidly during early structure formation, reaches content-specific operating ranges, and gradually returns toward a more balanced regime during late acoustic refinement. Speech and SFX maintain higher evidence reliance than music and singing over much of the trajectory, while mixed scenes lie between these regimes. Thus, the model does not apply a single fixed prior–evidence interpolation to all audio. Instead, it adjusts evidence reliance as a function of both acoustic content and diffusion phase, consistent with the intended role of the conflict gate. \n\n 5 Conclusion \n\n This paper presented SonicWeave, a unified flow-matching model for generating speech, singing, music, sound effects, and their mixtures with a single set of parameters. The central premise of this work is that conditional computation in unified audio must operate at two coupled scales: it must accommodate differences across acoustic domains while adapting to heterogeneous regions within the same scene. CPE-MoE addresses this requirement by routing temporally contiguous audio chunks through a learned combination of a global text-and-diffusion prior and local acoustic evidence, while preserving a shared conditioning pathway for text and time tokens. \n\n Across public TTS, text-to-audio, and text-to-music benchmarks, SonicWeave consistently improves over matched Dense and token-routed MoE controls. On the Complex-Scene suite, the principal gain is compositional: SonicWeave improves semantic adherence and joint request realization while maintaining perceptual quality comparable to the parameter-matched Base-MoE.\nQuantitatively, it reduces TTA FAD by 30.9% over Dense and improves Complex-Scene MOS-R by 0.18 over Base-MoE and by more than 1.2 points over the strongest external baseline.\nThe learned expert assignments and conflict-gate trajectories also vary with content, layer depth, and diffusion phase, providing evidence that the additional capacity is used conditionally rather than uniformly. \n\n Targeted studies reported in the appendix further compare the learned gate with fixed prior–evidence averaging and vary the routing chunk size. Fixed fusion degrades all reported task groups, while increasing the chunk size from 4 to 8 weakens most metrics, supporting adaptive fusion and relatively fine-grained chunk routing. \n\n These findings support chunk-level prior–evidence routing as a useful intermediate granularity for unified audio generation. At the same time, the current evaluation is limited to short-form generation, a two-speaker structured interface, and non-exhaustive routing ablations. Extending the approach to longer scenes, richer speaker interactions, and explicit temporal plans remains an important direction. \n\n References \n\n [1] \n A. Agostinelli, T. I. Denk, Z. Borsos, J. H. Engel, M. Verzetti, A. Caillon, Q. Huang, A. Jansen, A. Roberts, M. Tagliasacchi, M. Sharifi, N. Zeghidour, and C. H. Frank (2023) \n\n MusicLM: generating music from text .\n\n CoRR abs/2301.11325 .\n\n External Links: Document \n\n Cited by: §4.1 .\n\n [2] \n P. Anastassiou, J. Chen, J. Chen, Y. Chen, Z. Chen, Z. Chen, J. Cong, L. Deng, C. Ding, L. Gao, M. Gong, P. Huang, Q. Huang, Z. Huang, Y. Huo, D. Jia, C. Li, F. Li, H. Li, J. Li, X. Li, X. Li, L. Liu, S. Liu, S. Liu, X. Liu, Y. Liu, Z. Liu, L. Lu, J. Pan, X. Wang, Y. Wang, Y. Wang, Z. Wei, J. Wu, C. Yao, Y. Yang, Y. Yi, J. Zhang, Q. Zhang, S. Zhang, W. Zhang, Y. Zhang, Z. Zhao, D. Zhong, and X. Zhuang (2024) \n\n Seed-TTS: a family of high-quality versatile speech generation models .\n\n CoRR abs/2406.02430 .\n\n External Links: Document \n","numLines":500,"startLine":400,"totalLines":1960}}}
