diobox: unified audio generation with natural language prompts .\n1270\t\n1271\t CoRR abs/2312.15821 .\n1272\t\n1273\t External Links: Document \n1274\t\n1275\t Cited by: §1 ,\n1276\t §2.1 .\n1277\t\n1278\t [37] \n1279\t P. Wang, S. Bai, S. Tan, S. Wang, Z. Fan, J. Bai, K. Chen, X. Liu, J. Wang, W. Ge, Y. Fan, K. Dang, M. Du, X. Ren, R. Men, D. Liu, C. Zhou, J. Zhou, and J. Lin (2024) \n1280\t\n1281\t Qwen2-VL: enhancing vision-language model’s perception of the world at any resolution .\n1282\t\n1283\t CoRR abs/2409.12191 .\n1284\t\n1285\t External Links: Document \n1286\t\n1287\t Cited by: §3.3 .\n1288\t\n1289\t [38] \n1290\t Y. Wang, Z. Ji, P. Cai, X. Li, H. Zheng, Z. Song, Z. Liu, C. Zhang, and P. Wan (2026) \n1291\t\n1292\t SegTune: structured and fine-grained control for song generation .\n1293\t\n1294\t In Proceedings of the 64th Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers) ,\n1295\t\n1296\t pp. 12883–12897 .\n1297\t\n1298\t External Links: Document \n1299\t\n1300\t Cited by: Table 1 .\n1301\t\n1302\t [39] \n1303\t J. Xu, Z. Guo, J. He, H. Hu, T. He, S. Bai, K. Chen, J. Wang, Y. Fan, K. Dang, B. Zhang, X. Wang, Y. Chu, and J. Lin (2025) \n1304\t\n1305\t Qwen2.5-Omni technical report .\n1306\t\n1307\t CoRR abs/2503.20215 .\n1308\t\n1309\t External Links: Document \n1310\t\n1311\t Cited by: Table 1 .\n1312\t\n1313\t [40] \n1314\t X. Xu, J. Mei, Z. Zheng, Y. Tao, Z. Xie, Y. Zhang, H. Liu, Y. Wu, M. Yan, W. Wu, C. Zhang, and M. Wu (2025) \n1315\t\n1316\t UniFlow-Audio: unified flow matching for audio generation from omni-modalities .\n1317\t\n1318\t CoRR abs/2509.24391 .\n1319\t\n1320\t External Links: Document \n1321\t\n1322\t Cited by: Table 1 .\n1323\t\n1324\t [41] \n1325\t D. Yang, J. Tian, X. Tan, R. Huang, S. Liu, H. Guo, X. Chang, J. Shi, S. Zhao, J. Bian, Z. Zhao, X. Wu, and H. M. Meng (2024) \n1326\t\n1327\t UniAudio: towards universal audio generation with large language models .\n1328\t\n1329\t In Proceedings of the 41st International Conference on Machine Learning ,\n1330\t\n1331\t pp. 56422–56447 .\n1332\t\n1333\t Cited by: §1 ,\n1334\t §2.1 ,\n1335\t Table 1 .\n1336\t\n1337\t Appendix A Architecture and Objective Details \n1338\t\n1339\t A.1 Phase-Aware Text Pooling and AdaLN Conditioning \n1340\t\n1341\t Let 𝐄 ∈ ℝ B × L × d E \\mathbf{E}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{B\\times L\\times d_{E}} be the frozen text-encoder states, where d E = 4096 d_{E}=4096 , and let 𝐦 ∈ { 0 , 1 } B × L \\mathbf{m}\\in\\{0,1\\}^{B\\times L} be the caption-validity mask. The diffusion-time and segment-start embeddings are denoted by 𝐭 \\mathbf{t} and 𝐬 \\mathbf{s} , respectively. We first construct the phase condition \n1342\t\n1343\t 𝐮 = 𝐭 + 𝐬 ∈ ℝ B × d c , d c = 512 . \\mathbf{u}=\\mathbf{t}+\\mathbf{s}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{B\\times d_{c}},\\qquad d_{c}=512. \n1344\t\n1345\t (17) \n1346\t\n1347\t Rather than mean-pooling all text tokens, SonicWeave uses 𝐮 \\mathbf{u} as a query that changes with the generation phase: \n1348\t\n1349\t 𝐪 \\displaystyle\\mathbf{q} \n1350\t = W q ​ 𝐮 , \\displaystyle=W_{q}\\mathbf{u}, \n1351\t 𝐊 \\displaystyle\\mathbf{K} \n1352\t = W k ​ 𝐄 , \\displaystyle=W_{k}\\mathbf{E}, \n1353\t\n1354\t (18) \n1355\t\n1356\t a i \\displaystyle a_{i} \n1357\t = 𝐪 ⊤ ​ 𝐊 i d E , \\displaystyle=\\frac{\\mathbf{q}^{\\top}\\mathbf{K}_{i}}{\\sqrt{d_{E}}}, \n1358\t α i \\displaystyle\\alpha_{i} \n1359\t = exp ⁡ ( a i ) ​ m i ∑ r = 1 L exp ⁡ ( a r ) ​ m r + ϵ , \\displaystyle=\\frac{\\exp(a_{i})\\,m_{i}}{\\sum_{r=1}^{L}\\exp(a_{r})\\,m_{r}+\\epsilon}, \n1360\t\n1361\t 𝐞 ¯ \\displaystyle\\bar{\\mathbf{e}} \n1362\t = ∑ i = 1 L α i ​ 𝐄 i , \\displaystyle=\\sum_{i=1}^{L}\\alpha_{i}\\mathbf{E}_{i}, \n1363\t 𝐜 \\displaystyle\\mathbf{c} \n1364\t = 𝐮 + W g ​ 𝐞 ¯ . \\displaystyle=\\mathbf{u}+W_{g}\\bar{\\mathbf{e}}. \n1365\t\n1366\t The implementation masks invalid text positions before the softmax, multiplies the resulting weights by 𝐦 \\mathbf{m} once more, and renormalizes. Consequently, padding contributes neither to 𝐞 ¯ \\bar{\\mathbf{e}} nor to its gradients. If all text tokens are absent (the caption-dropped classifier-free guidance branch), 𝐞 ¯ \\bar{\\mathbf{e}} is set to zero and 𝐜 = 𝐮 \\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{u} . \n1367\t\n1368\t The global condition 𝐜 \\mathbf{c} is supplied to the final AdaLN-Zero layer that normalizes the audio-token states before the velocity projection. Token-level text–audio correspondence is still modeled through joint self-attention; Eq. ( 18 ) is a complementary global path. Its phase-dependent query lets the model alter which parts of a structured caption are globally emphasized during generation, while the residual 𝐮 \\mathbf{u} preserves explicit diffusion and segment-position information. \n1369\t\n1370\t A.2 Backbone and CPE-MoE Configuration \n1371\t\n1372\t Table  6 lists the configuration used by the reported SonicWeave model. The model consumes a sequence consisting of one time token, up to 200 text tokens in the reported configuration, and at most 256 audio latent frames. Text and audio positions use independent coordinate ranges under rotary positional embedding, so an audio position does not change when the caption length changes. The audio input projection receives the concatenation of the current noised latent and the masked reference latent; the velocity head predicts 64 channels per latent frame. \n1373\t\n1374\t Table 6: Backbone and CPE-MoE configuration. The text encoder is frozen; all listed DiT and routing components are trained jointly. \n1375\t\n1376\t Component \n1377\t\n1378\t Configuration \n1379\t\n1380\t Text encoder \n1381\t\n1382\t Frozen Qwen3-VL-8B  [ 3 ] text encoder; d E = 4096 d_{E}=4096 \n1383\t\n1384\t DiT width / depth \n1385\t\n1386\t d = 2048 d=2048 / 16 transformer blocks \n1387\t\n1388\t Attention \n1389\t\n1390\t 32 heads; head width 64; SDPA attention \n1391\t\n1392\t Dense FFN width \n1393\t\n1394\t 4 ​ d = 8192 4d=8192 ; SiLU activation \n1395\t\n1396\t Text-token limit \n1397\t\n1398\t 200 tokens \n1399\t\n1400\t Max audio length \n1401\t\n1402\t 256 latent frames \n1403\t\n1404\t Latent channels \n1405\t\n1406\t 64 channels \n1407\t\n1408\t AdaLN width \n1409\t\n1410\t 512 \n1411\t\n1412\t Routed blocks \n1413\t\n1414\t Final 4 of 16 blocks \n1415\t\n1416\t Routed experts \n1417\t\n1418\t 4 experts per routed block \n1419\t\n1420\t Sparse selection \n1421\t\n1422\t Top-2 experts per valid chunk \n1423\t\n1424\t Chunk length \n1425\t\n1426\t C = 4 C=4 latent frames \n1427\t\n1428\t CL Loss \n1429\t\n1430\t α CL \\alpha_{\\mathrm{CL}} =0.1 \n1431\t\n1432\t Load Balancing \n1433\t\n1434\t α MoE \\alpha_{\\mathrm{MoE}} =0.05 \n1435\t\n1436\t Each routed block retains one shared FFN. The time token and all text tokens are processed only by this shared FFN. Only audio tokens enter the routed path, which prevents the routing decision from directly modifying the condition-token pathway. For a valid chunk, top-2 posterior probabilities are renormalized to sum to one, broadcast to all frames in the chunk, and used to weight the corresponding expert outputs. Expert outputs are computed on the original frame states, not on pooled chunk states; pooling is used only to make a routing decision. \n1437\t\n1438\t The routed experts use zero-initialized output projections. Thus, the routed contribution begins near zero and is introduced smoothly as optimization learns both the experts and the router. For audio tokens, the shared and sparse paths are combined with equal weights, 1 2 ​ 𝐨 shared + 1 2 ​ 𝐨 sparse \\tfrac{1}{2}\\mathbf{o}_{\\mathrm{shared}}+\\tfrac{1}{2}\\mathbf{o}_{\\mathrm{sparse}} . Padding frames and padding-only chunks are masked before dispatch and are excluded from all router statistics. \n1439\t\n1440\t Appendix B Structured Prompt Interface \n1441\t\n1442\t B.1 Schema and Field Semantics \n1443\t\n1444\t SonicWeave uses one structured text-conditioning interface for all text-to-audio generation modes. A prompt consists of a short natural-language summary followed by a sparse set of XML-like fields (shown in Table  7 ). Fields with no applicable information are omitted rather than filled with generic text. This is important: absence is meaningful for, for example, instrumental music, which should not receive an invented transcript or vocal attribute. \n1445\t\n1446\t Table 7: Structured prompt fields. The interface describes requested audible attributes only. \n1447\t\n1448\t Field \n1449\t\n1450\t Value type \n1451\t\n1452\t Semantics \n1453\t\n1454\t type \n1455\t\n1456\t categorical \n1457\t\n1458\t Dominant audio mode: speech, singing, music, sound effects, or mixed. mixed is used only when two or more of speech, singing, music, and sound effects are explicitly requested; it does not itself imply speech. \n1459\t\n1460\t lang \n1461\t\n1462\t language code \n1463\t\n1464\t Language of intelligible speech or singing. It is omitted for non-vocal audio. \n1465\t\n1466\t speech \n1467\t\n1468\t verbatim text \n1469\t\n1470\t Spoken linguistic content, including ordered dialogue turns separated by slashes when applicable. \n1471\t\n1472\t lyrics \n1473\t\n1474\t text \n1475\t\n1476\t Song lyrics content when provided. \n1477\t\n1478\t speaker_count \n1479\t\n1480\t categorical \n1481\t\n1482\t Number of foreground speakers or vocalists when applicable: 1 or 2. Dialogue scenes support at most two speakers. \n1483\t\n1484\t relation \n1485\t\n1486\t short phrase \n1487\t\n1488\t Speaker roles, turn-taking relation, or foreground interaction, such as a host and guest or two commuters. \n1489\t\n1490\t vocal \n1491\t\n1492\t short phrase \n1493\t\n1494\t Speaker identity descriptors, vocal timbre, delivery, emotion, accent, or recording coloration relevant to the foreground voice. \n1495\t\n1496\t music \n1497\t\n1498\t short phrase \n1499\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6b970116-bc68-429c-9d83-8e23452428b2","uuid":"d598c34a-b76d-4734-8c1c-a47cfdd50c88","timestamp":"2026-08-11T06:47:30.816Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/sonicweave.txt","content":" Cited by: §4.1 .\n\n [3] \n S. Bai, Y. Cai, R. Chen, K. Chen, X. Chen, Z. Cheng, L. Deng, W. Ding, C. Gao, C. Ge, et al. (2025) \n\n Qwen3-vl technical report .\n\n arXiv preprint arXiv:2511.21631 .\n\n Cited by: Table 6 .\n\n [4] \n Boson AI (2025) \n\n Higgs Audio V2: redefining expressiveness in audio generation .\n\n Note: GitHub repositoryBoson AI Higgs Audio project \n\n Cited by: Table 1 .\n\n [5] \n Y. Chen, Z. Niu, Z. Ma, K. Deng, C. Wang, J. Zhao, K. Yu, and X. Chen (2025) \n\n F5-TTS: a fairytaler that fakes fluent and faithful speech with flow matching .\n\n In Proceedings of the 63rd Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers) ,\n\n pp. 6255–6271 .\n\n Cited by: §1 ,\n §2.1 ,\n Table 1 .\n\n [6] \n A. Conneau, D. Kiela, H. Schwenk, L. Barrault, and A. Bordes (2017) \n\n Supervised learning of universal sentence representations from natural language inference data .\n\n In Proceedings of the 2017 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing ,\n\n pp. 670–680 .\n\n External Links: Document \n\n Cited by: §3.4 .\n\n [7] \n J. Copet, F. Kreuk, I. Gat, T. Remez, D. Kant, G. Synnaeve, Y. Adi, and A. Défossez (2023) \n\n Simple and controllable music generation .\n\n Advances in neural information processing systems 36 , pp. 47704–47720 .\n\n Cited by: Table 1 .\n\n [8] \n Z. Du, Y. Wang, Q. Chen, X. Shi, X. Lv, T. Zhao, Z. Gao, Y. Yang, C. Gao, H. Wang, F. Yu, H. Liu, Z. Sheng, Y. Gu, C. Deng, W. Wang, S. Zhang, Z. Yan, and J. Zhou (2024) \n\n CosyVoice 2: scalable streaming speec